00:04:12.336 --> 00:04:14.150
JOHN LIU: Thank you so much.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:15.390
Hello, everyone.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:17.220
I'm so, so excited to be here.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.852
Thank you again, Saif
and Kirky, for having me.

00:04:20.852 --> 00:04:22.310
What I'd like to
share today really

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:27.360
stems from, you could say, our
experience of the past couple

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.345
of months of transitioning.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:33.170
We were all shocked.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:36.170
I still remember two months
ago when MIT pulled the plug,

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:38.540
and we realized
everything's going remote.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:40.760
And we could think
through a lot of how

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.940
we were going to transition,
but we didn't really

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:45.590
know how to think
about transitioning

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:47.480
the lab in hands-on activities.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:49.090
That really seemed
like a black hole.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:50.340
We didn't know where to start.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.790
So we've been-- I've
been in the trenches

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:54.650
now for a couple
months, and I'd love

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:58.040
to share a little bit about what
our department has been up to

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:00.740
and share with you some
of what we've learned.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:04.730
And to start, so our
department, this term

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:08.930
we're teaching 30 labs
in project-based classes.

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:12.980
And we can really parse
all of the classes

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:15.440
into three buckets
using a very--

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.700
by asking a very
simple question--

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:21.340
where does the model exist?

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.520
Does the model exist on campus?

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:27.100
Does the model exist at home?

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:30.880
Or does the model exist
in the virtual space?

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:34.750
And to put some feet to this,
let me give you some examples.

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:37.980
So when the model--

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.620
if the model exists
on campus, usually we

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:44.040
found it's a scenario where the
equipment is fairly expensive,

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:45.840
or it's dangerous,
or it's hard to move.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:47.430
It's large and heavy.

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:51.570
And it also was in cases when
the staff could be present.

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:54.240
So we had two types
of cases where

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.800
the model exists on campus.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:00.210
One, you can pre-record
your own lab experiments

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:01.650
and talk through them.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:03.510
But then, once you
have the recording,

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.160
you host a synchronous
discussion.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:08.070
So you show your own
work, recorded work.

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:09.660
And you pause it
at different parts.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:13.650
And you ask your students,
what should we do now, or what

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:16.810
about these different options,
or did you notice this or that?

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:18.480
There's also real-time.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:21.660
So you notice that
I'm showing an SEM.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.640
We literally outfitted an SEM.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.650
It was already controlled
by a desktop computer.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:29.790
But then we set it up so that
it could be controlled remote.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:31.740
So students could control--

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:34.230
whether they're in
Indiana to India,

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:36.180
they can control the
computer and, therefore,

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.395
perform SEM experiments
from anywhere in the world.

00:06:39.395 --> 00:06:40.770
There's another
iteration of this

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:44.580
where perhaps an instructor
puts a GoPro on their head

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:48.510
and has different
views from cameras.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:51.638
And the instructors basically,
they act as the hands.

00:06:51.638 --> 00:06:53.680
And they ask the students,
what should I do next?

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:55.380
And the students are
the ones who instruct.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:57.870
And they talk through different
experiments or fabrication

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:00.600
processes.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:02.670
When the model exists
at home, usually we're

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:06.300
talking about a scenario
where you can significantly

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.670
or meaningfully
reduce the complexity

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.960
and that you can handle
the logistics of shipping

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:17.310
and also the safety of students
actually interacting over these

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:20.880
without direct presence
from the staff.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:23.910
So we had homegrown
type experiments

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:25.830
where students had
to go, and they

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.870
had to prototype
their own terrarium

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:31.440
as we were thinking about
food scarcity in the world.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:34.530
We teach design by play.

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:37.770
And so students were using
foam, simple materials like foam

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:40.960
and balsa wood, to put together
prototypes of board games.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.250
Here's one on
playing with islands.

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:47.040
So here, you're collecting
materials from or to home,

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:49.530
and then you're
assembling them at home.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:54.490
Or we're sending kits directly
to the homes of each student.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:59.700
So on the top here, we actually
sent commercial CNC Engravers

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:02.910
to each student that was taking
a Precision Machining Design

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:03.870
course.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:08.460
Or for control and
dynamics, we sent--

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:12.090
we assembled kits, literally
from the contracting

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:14.820
the work of laser engraving,
and cutting aluminum struts,

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:18.420
and having vendors
send us wheels

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:20.820
that we could assemble
subassemblies and then send

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:24.540
them to students where they had
to assemble the subassemblies

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.690
and then code their own
microcontroller to learn

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:31.560
about feedback control and how
a Segway robot kit can still

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:38.570
remain and move, stand still,
when it's being controlled.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:41.900
And then finally, if the model
exists in the virtual world,

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:44.510
often it's a scenario
where you already

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:47.360
have pre-existing virtual
resources that you

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.090
can translate to have
comparable activities that you

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:51.710
were doing on campus.

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:55.230
So especially for our
design or project courses,

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:57.860
here's a case where students
are designing robots.

00:08:57.860 --> 00:08:59.960
And they usually
have to CAD anyway,

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:01.760
but they move very
quickly to take

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:05.720
that CAD to produce
blueprints to fabricate them

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:06.860
in the machine shop.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:10.000
But now, with the extra time
and space we're allotted,

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.670
we've really focused on not just
CAD for form but the function,

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:16.190
focusing on the mating
between different parts,

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:19.430
focusing on tolerances
between how the parts fit

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:21.350
together and move.

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:24.800
And then, instead of then
prototyping and then testing,

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:27.440
does this prototype actually
fit the functional requirements

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:30.640
of my design, all of that can
be moved to the virtual space

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:31.890
if you have software packages.

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:36.008
So here's an example where,
in the class that I teach,

00:09:36.008 --> 00:09:37.550
students have to
mass-produce yo-yos.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:40.070
And instead of doing
that, here we have them,

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:42.740
through a software
package called Moldflow,

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:45.890
you're actually simulating the
flow of the plastic, the molten

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:48.560
plastic, into the yo-yo
molds that they already

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:51.520
designed originally
for the machine shop.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:53.540
And then, instead
of actually thinking

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:57.650
through, how might I
rearrange the current machine

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:01.370
shop or a factory floor, we have
them simulate the manufacturing

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:03.355
system through Tecnomatix.

00:10:03.355 --> 00:10:05.480
And there's a number of
different software packages

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:07.620
out there.

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.448
And so you have the practical
scenarios and constraints

00:10:11.448 --> 00:10:12.740
that will guide your decisions.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:15.140
But really, you want to think
about the other side, too,

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:16.520
the pedagogical side.

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:19.550
How do you link
the learning goals?

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:21.830
Really, that should
be the first question.

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:24.170
What are your learning goals,
and how do you prioritize?

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:25.730
And what do you
want the students

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:29.060
to get out of that lab
or hands-on activity?

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:32.450
Is it the synthesis
of a phenomenon

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:34.590
on nano, micro, macro scale?

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:36.920
Is it a design for prototyping?

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:38.510
Is it design for manufacturing?

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:43.460
Is it the analysis or
characterization of a process?

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:46.640
What do you want your students
to get out of an activity?

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:48.290
And then after that,
think through some

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:50.930
of the unique aspects
of hands-on learning,

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:54.420
the reasons why you wanted
them to have hands-on learning.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:57.470
So for example, hands-on
learning is good because you

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:03.070
can't hand wave through all of--

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:03.810
hand wave away.

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:06.550
You just can see how those
cause outputs directly.

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:09.130
Or is it that you want
that physical layer

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:13.210
to break the often what's
bifurcated in students?

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:15.580
They do homework, and then
they throw all that out

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:17.260
when they look at
the real world.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:20.530
You want to break that by
adding that physical layer.

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:22.750
Or is it there's
something special

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:27.130
that students learn when they
can feel and they can touch?

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:28.690
So how do you link --

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:30.170
what are your learning goals?

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:32.140
And how do you link
these different aspects

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:33.860
of hands-on learning to it?

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:35.498
Because what you
need to do is you

00:11:35.498 --> 00:11:36.790
need to think about both sides.

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:38.830
You need to think about
the practical reasons,

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:40.930
the scenarios and constraints--

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:44.830
and I gave you an example,
different examples of those--

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:46.990
and also the pedagogical ones.

00:11:46.990 --> 00:11:49.940
So each of these are going to
have different characteristics.

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:54.130
So if the model exists on
campus, then in general,

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:56.820
when you transition,
you're going

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:59.410
to see that you're
able to preserve most

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:02.080
of the real-world
aspects, because that

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:05.890
was the original context that
you designed those activities.

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:08.830
You can also capture the
same level of complexity.

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:11.590
But then people don't
feel things necessarily.

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:14.920
And often, there's
also a decreased amount

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:16.210
of interaction.

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:19.000
I mean, even in the case
with the remote control,

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:22.940
the staff had to load
the sample into the SEM,

00:12:22.940 --> 00:12:26.420
the scanning electron
microscope, for example.

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:29.680
If the model exists
in the home, then you

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:31.780
can capture a lot of
the different aspects,

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:34.660
but you do have to significantly
reduce the complexity so

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:37.120
that it can be shipped
over or so that students

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:40.740
can handle them by themselves
without you next to them.

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:44.480
And then finally, if the model
exists in the virtual space,

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:47.180
a lot of software packages--
research, commercial--

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:50.750
they can be quite complex, and
there's a lot of input output.

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:54.740
But you may miss
the physical layer

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:58.730
because they're seeing something
that's simulated or analyzed.

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:03.860
And then finally, there's
a difference in this piece

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:07.430
that you have to think through,
where is that complexity?

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:14.665
So these are the different areas
that we found really came up

00:13:14.665 --> 00:13:17.290
in our discussions when we were
thinking about that transition.

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:19.010
So first, safety.

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:24.033
If your model exists in campus,
then staff have to be there.

00:13:24.033 --> 00:13:25.450
And you have to
think through, how

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:28.780
are you going to social
distance while you operate

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:30.280
the different activities?

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:32.200
Safety means something
different for home.

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:34.158
How are students going
to independently operate

00:13:34.158 --> 00:13:36.280
the materials or equipment?

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:38.200
For the budget,
most of your budget

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:40.030
when the model
exists on campus is

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:43.930
going to go to setting up
whatever equipment that you had

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:45.640
and now transferring that over.

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:48.160
So if it's for remote control,
the hardware or the software

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:49.090
setup.

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:52.790
When it exists in the home,
kit materials and the shipping.

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:55.750
And then finally,
the software licenses

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:57.750
was probably the main--

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:00.100
what composed of the
lion's share of the budget

00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:01.600
when we were talking
about the model

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:05.060
line in the virtual world.

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:07.070
You'll also be focusing
on different things.

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:10.400
A lot of our workload
in the first category

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:14.820
really went to going to set
up this real-time instruction

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:16.850
and real-time feedback.

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:18.680
If the model exists
in home, there

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:21.560
was incredible time spent
on the assembly of the kits

00:14:21.560 --> 00:14:23.600
or making sure that
the instructions could

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:26.730
be followed when you're
not physically present.

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:29.270
And then finally, if the work--

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:31.820
if the model is in the
virtual space, a lot of time

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:36.320
was spent on, one, the staff,
us learning the new software.

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:37.880
We had to translate
the activities

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:40.190
into the software
package itself,

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:43.358
and then working with IT.

00:14:43.358 --> 00:14:44.900
Your students are
going to experience

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:47.180
different limitations in
these different models,

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:49.860
as well, in these different
locations, as well.

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:51.680
Now obviously,
there's a lot of talk

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:55.790
about how students experience
bandwidth issues with Zoom

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:58.400
now, for example, that there
could be fuzzier audio.

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:02.180
But that's somewhat applicable
through all of these.

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:04.950
What I mean internet connection
when the model exists on campus

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:07.820
is real-time control has
an additional bandwidth

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:08.450
requirement.

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:10.490
And how are you going
to control something?

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:13.910
And if there's lag,
how is a student going

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:17.000
to see it in time to react?

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:20.150
When the model exists at home,
you have to think through,

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:22.370
how are the students going
to receive these packages?

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:25.100
Do they have the tools
to fabricate or measure?

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:27.620
And then, is there
even a stable space

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:29.450
where they can carry
out these activities

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:31.590
that they want to carry out?

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:34.070
When the model
exists in the cloud

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:36.380
or in the virtual, space
you have to think through,

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:39.350
do the students have the
personal computing resources

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:42.680
necessary to install
the software, the latest

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:44.720
and the greatest that
you want them to use?

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:46.137
Or is this something
that you have

00:15:46.137 --> 00:15:49.600
to contract out, for
example, to Amazon computing?

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:51.050
But there's things that--

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:52.760
there's unique
opportunities that

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:54.950
are associated with all three.

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:58.250
We found that with labs
that are on campus, often

00:15:58.250 --> 00:16:00.680
the person who is closest to
whatever you're demonstrating

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:03.902
gets a front row seat, but
everyone else, especially

00:16:03.902 --> 00:16:05.360
the people all the
way in the back,

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:06.950
they don't really see anything.

00:16:06.950 --> 00:16:10.410
When you share screen,
everyone sees the same thing.

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:14.570
And so there's a uniform
experience of the experiment

00:16:14.570 --> 00:16:17.700
that you want them to
analyze and to consider.

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:21.200
On the learning opportunities
for these the model

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:24.110
laying in the home, there's
somewhat of an increased sense

00:16:24.110 --> 00:16:25.730
of independence and agency.

00:16:25.730 --> 00:16:27.950
Are they collecting the
materials around their home?

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:29.390
Are they walking
outside the home

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:31.920
and looking at the
world around them?

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:35.330
They have to connect a lot more
to their personal and physical

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:36.720
context.

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:38.450
And then finally, I
got a lot of feedback

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:42.550
from instructors that there
was a deeper sense of,

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:45.873
there was a tighter loop when
the model exists in virtual,

00:16:45.873 --> 00:16:46.790
and you're simulating.

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:49.190
There's a tighter loop
between the inputs

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:52.160
that you're putting in and
then directly, how does that

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:56.810
cause changes in function,
or form, or phenomenon?

00:16:56.810 --> 00:17:01.250
And so students were much
more able to really think

00:17:01.250 --> 00:17:05.510
through the deeper contextual
understanding of whatever model

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:09.300
that you wanted them to think
about in the first place.

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:12.760
So I hope that framework helps
you to think through your own

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:16.200
just to combine pedagogical
and practical considerations.

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:16.950
Thank you so much.

00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:19.972
And I'm looking forward
to the questions.

00:17:19.972 --> 00:17:21.290
SAIF RAYYAN: Thank you, John.

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:25.369
This was a great introduction
to the different strategies

00:17:25.369 --> 00:17:29.840
that you can actually
use at different levels.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:33.470
I love your discussion
around the learning

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:35.270
and the pedagogical value.

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:38.660
What I'm getting out of this
is that probably the most

00:17:38.660 --> 00:17:44.090
efficient is a combination of
all these methods in some form,

00:17:44.090 --> 00:17:46.520
because some of them
provide opportunities

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:47.330
in different areas.

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:49.640
And one of the things that
we've been talking about

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:52.850
is that, even after
this crisis is over,

00:17:52.850 --> 00:17:55.580
we will probably continue
using some of these methods

00:17:55.580 --> 00:17:58.770
because they have
their own strengths.

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:01.040
And this is a good
transition into the rest

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:04.430
of the presentations
where we will focus more

00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:08.810
on the virtual and the remote
part of the experience,

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:13.710
which where we're all
using more often now.

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:18.770
So the next speaker, next
panelist, is Peter Bohacek.

00:18:18.770 --> 00:18:20.450
I'm so excited to
have Peter here.

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:23.180
I met Peter years ago at
the American Association

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:25.810
of Physics Teacher.

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:27.860
Peter is a physics
teacher in Minnesota,

00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:30.470
but he's also the Co-founder
of Pivot Interactives.

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:32.930
He comes from an
industry background.

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:37.490
And first time he showed
me his interactive videos,

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:38.630
I was a believer.

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:42.170
I was like, this is
a fantastic resource.

00:18:42.170 --> 00:18:45.221
I've not seen in a video--

00:18:45.221 --> 00:18:49.540
video for me is very static.

00:18:49.540 --> 00:18:51.440
There is not much engagement.

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:53.870
Sure, you can show
something that's fancy.

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:57.830
But he is able to use
the videos in a very

00:18:57.830 --> 00:18:59.500
engaging, interactive
way, and he's

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:01.356
going to tell us more about it.

00:19:01.356 --> 00:19:02.640
PETER BOHACEK: Great.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:03.330
Thank you.

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:04.380
And John, thank you.

00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:07.110
That introduction is
really a good overview.

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:13.470
And so it's a nice segue into
exactly what the affordance is

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:15.900
of interactive video.

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:18.150
And I particularly
appreciate the part

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:21.120
about deciding what the
learning goals are, because

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:23.490
for laboratory experiences
and for all teaching, that's

00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:24.815
the starting point.

00:19:24.815 --> 00:19:26.690
What are you hoping the
students to get out--

00:19:26.690 --> 00:19:28.230
are going to get out of it?

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:32.800
So I'd like to share
my screen and show

00:19:32.800 --> 00:19:35.380
about interactive video.

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:40.570
So interactive video, as
Saif said, video in general

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:43.570
can be a passive learning tool,
not that it can't be effective,

00:19:43.570 --> 00:19:45.760
but with interactivity--

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:47.710
I'll just show you a
quick example and then

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:51.130
some of the different ways
that interactive video can

00:19:51.130 --> 00:19:55.450
be used remotely and for
face to face learning

00:19:55.450 --> 00:19:57.303
and for different
subject areas, as well.

00:19:57.303 --> 00:19:58.970
So I'll just start
with this video here.

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:01.810
This is a high-speed
video filmed

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:04.610
of a stream of water, a
laminar flow fountain.

00:20:04.610 --> 00:20:06.520
It doesn't look like
it's moving, but it is.

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:09.160
And a metal blade is
going to come and sever

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:11.890
this and cause a gap.

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:14.800
And so one of the first
things about interactive video

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:16.330
is it's engaging for students.

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:18.663
And this is something that
we've researched quite a bit.

00:20:18.663 --> 00:20:21.580
And immediately, students
are fascinated by the motion

00:20:21.580 --> 00:20:24.850
of the water, why it's so--

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:27.610
appears not to be moving
before and then suddenly

00:20:27.610 --> 00:20:29.260
realize it is.

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:31.870
So then, to make it
interactive, what we have here

00:20:31.870 --> 00:20:33.620
is a set of measurement tools.

00:20:33.620 --> 00:20:37.990
So here, the students
can have a ruler

00:20:37.990 --> 00:20:41.170
that they can use and
rotate to make measurements,

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:44.050
a protractor, stopwatch.

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:48.450
And so they are
[INAUDIBLE] decisions

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:50.855
about how and what
they're going to measure.

00:20:50.855 --> 00:20:52.230
And in this
particular case, what

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:53.605
we're going to
ask students to do

00:20:53.605 --> 00:20:58.140
is apply a model
for how objects move

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:00.280
under the influence of gravity.

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:05.070
And so they are going to
make a prediction about where

00:21:05.070 --> 00:21:09.150
they think this gap in the water
is going to land as it travels

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:10.810
along out of the screen.

00:21:10.810 --> 00:21:14.790
And so after they do
that, this is an example

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:18.300
of how we implement interactive
video in our Pivot Interactives

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:21.000
platform, where we have
a series of questions.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:22.890
In this case, the students
make a prediction.

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:25.800
And then after they
do, this is exactly

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:28.260
the same event that
you saw up above,

00:21:28.260 --> 00:21:30.210
the same laminar flow
fountain recorded

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:31.410
at exactly the same time.

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:36.950
You'll see the saw
blade pass through

00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:38.690
and create that same gap.

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:41.000
But now the students
can find out

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:43.790
whether the prediction that they
made about where this is going

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:46.060
to land is accurate or not.

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:47.810
And also John mentioned,
one of the things

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:50.070
about the complexities,
the details,

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:52.502
the deviations from
simplified models.

00:21:52.502 --> 00:21:53.960
And here, students
really get to do

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:56.860
that because the width
of the gap grows.

00:21:56.860 --> 00:21:59.270
And so an added
question is, why?

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:01.460
And so, when you
made your prediction,

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:04.100
would it be more accurate to
say that it's for the leading

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:05.490
edge or the trailing edge?

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:07.640
And so immediately,
we get students

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:10.580
asking the kinds of questions
that, as educators, we

00:22:10.580 --> 00:22:15.350
hope they will, far beyond an
equation for projectile motion.

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:18.690
But, how do these things
look in the real world?

00:22:18.690 --> 00:22:20.990
So that's an example.

00:22:20.990 --> 00:22:23.240
Besides things that
are very slow motion,

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:25.430
we also can do apparatus
that would normally

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:29.180
be difficult for the
students to have access to.

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:32.180
So in this case, we're
going to study conservation

00:22:32.180 --> 00:22:33.062
of mechanical energy.

00:22:33.062 --> 00:22:34.770
So we'd like to have
a frictionless ramp.

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:38.720
And so we use a
superconducting disk that

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:40.890
sits above a magnetic ramp.

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:43.730
And so now this can move
with very, very low friction.

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:46.430
So the students can see,
in this case, what they're

00:22:46.430 --> 00:22:49.520
going to be asked to do is
develop a model, a model that

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:51.440
relates the release height.

00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:54.500
So here, they can select
a different release height

00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:58.330
and load a video, and then see,
how does that release height

00:22:58.330 --> 00:23:00.650
affect the speed at the bottom?

00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:04.940
So they'll make a little
speed trap here at the bottom,

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:06.970
use a stopwatch here.

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:08.970
So now they can measure
the speed at the bottom.

00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:11.460
And they can also have
other measurement tools

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:14.560
that they use to measure
the release height.

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:17.250
And so then they can build
a model here and find,

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:19.350
what is the mathematical
relationship

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:22.290
between the height from
which it's released

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:24.930
and the velocity at the bottom?

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.990
So John was talking
about the goals

00:23:27.990 --> 00:23:32.490
for, I think for a lot of people
as educators in science models,

00:23:32.490 --> 00:23:36.155
developing models, testing
models, applying models.

00:23:36.155 --> 00:23:37.530
And so in this
case, the students

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:41.020
are able to build a model
that finds that relationship.

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:42.570
I'll just quickly
show an example now

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:43.820
where they can test the model.

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:46.257
This is the same apparatus,
but now the video

00:23:46.257 --> 00:23:48.090
shows that the puck is
going to get flicked.

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:54.128
But the video ends before
the puck gets very far.

00:23:54.128 --> 00:23:56.670
And so the students are asked
this time to make a prediction.

00:23:56.670 --> 00:23:59.150
Where will that puck
end up, up the ramp?

00:23:59.150 --> 00:24:00.900
And after they make
the prediction, again,

00:24:00.900 --> 00:24:02.760
like we saw in
the last one, then

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:04.620
the video is
revealed that allows

00:24:04.620 --> 00:24:07.510
them to test whether
the prediction is right.

00:24:07.510 --> 00:24:12.150
The two examples that I've shown
are in introductory physics.

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:13.620
Oh, I suppose this one is, too.

00:24:13.620 --> 00:24:15.930
So this is an example
where, again, students

00:24:15.930 --> 00:24:16.890
may not have access to.

00:24:16.890 --> 00:24:19.740
In this case, what we
have is an authentic gold

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:21.960
medal from the
PyeongChang Olympics

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:25.140
and a fake gold medal from
the PyeongChang Olympics.

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:27.390
And both of them are subjected
to the same experiment.

00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:29.720
They're immersed
in a tank of water.

00:24:29.720 --> 00:24:31.650
And you can read
the scale reading.

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:34.660
And from that, the students
can determine not only

00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:37.500
which is the actual
gold medal, but what

00:24:37.500 --> 00:24:39.910
is a gold medal made out of?

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:41.460
So again, this is
the sort of thing

00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:43.110
that interactive
video allows students

00:24:43.110 --> 00:24:44.910
to see things they
may not otherwise

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:46.800
be able to interact with.

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:48.330
Also things that are very small.

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:50.670
In this case, this is
a chemistry activity

00:24:50.670 --> 00:24:53.220
where students can
explore the surface

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:54.810
tension from different liquids.

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:57.510
So these are the
jaws of a micrometer,

00:24:57.510 --> 00:24:59.040
and they're slowly stretched.

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:01.440
And this is a
droplet of pentane.

00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:06.120
There is an action figure,
a bobblehead of Marie Curie,

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:07.290
in the background.

00:25:07.290 --> 00:25:10.290
You can see the outline of her,
this beige background, and also

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:13.500
the image of her
focused in that droplet.

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:16.890
And so the students can use an
interactive measurement tool

00:25:16.890 --> 00:25:22.440
to measure and compare surface
tension of different liquids

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:25.320
and from that develop their
understanding of intermolecular

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:25.870
forces.

00:25:25.870 --> 00:25:27.537
So here, they have a
tutorial that helps

00:25:27.537 --> 00:25:29.550
them develop and test models.

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:32.970
And ultimately, each section
of this activity, they expand.

00:25:32.970 --> 00:25:35.710
There more and more liquids
they can do with it.

00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:37.710
Interactive video allows
you to have perspective

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:38.710
you wouldn't be able to.

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:43.500
So here's a shot of a traffic
circle recorded with a drone.

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:48.800
One of [AUDIO OUT] amazed by is
these drones are unbelievable.

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:50.450
The video is so stable.

00:25:50.450 --> 00:25:53.735
It looks like the drone
is bolted to the sky.

00:25:53.735 --> 00:25:55.610
And again, with interactive
measurement tools

00:25:55.610 --> 00:25:58.010
here, they could explore
coefficient of friction

00:25:58.010 --> 00:26:00.710
that must exist between
the car tires and the road.

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:07.300
Things that are too
toxic or dangerous.

00:26:07.300 --> 00:26:10.300
So here, students have
a choice of selecting

00:26:10.300 --> 00:26:13.600
different substances
and quantities.

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:17.860
And in this case, this is going
to be mixed with water, sodium

00:26:17.860 --> 00:26:18.930
hydroxide and water.

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:20.680
And then we also have
the ability for them

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:22.990
to not only lead a
temperature change

00:26:22.990 --> 00:26:26.980
but see an infrared compared
with [INAUDIBLE] what

00:26:26.980 --> 00:26:28.920
some of these reactions
are endothermic

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:29.800
and some exothermic.

00:26:29.800 --> 00:26:32.320
And so they've got
a visualization.

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:33.460
John mentioned the tactile.

00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:34.840
They miss that here.

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:37.180
They can't touch this liquid
and see that it's hot.

00:26:37.180 --> 00:26:40.210
On the other hand, I'm not sure
that we would want them to.

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:43.660
And then one last affordance
of interactive video

00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:46.250
that I'd like to point
out is time lapse.

00:26:46.250 --> 00:26:47.835
So whereas in
physics, we often have

00:26:47.835 --> 00:26:49.210
events that are
very short, so we

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:50.710
want to use slow
motion videography,

00:26:50.710 --> 00:26:55.610
and in others, in
biology we often have

00:26:55.610 --> 00:26:56.910
events that take a long time.

00:26:56.910 --> 00:26:59.900
So here is a 10-day
long time lapse

00:26:59.900 --> 00:27:03.650
of a terrarium that contains
some bean seeds and soil

00:27:03.650 --> 00:27:06.320
and water.

00:27:06.320 --> 00:27:09.200
We have a readout on top
of the carbon dioxide

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:11.530
level, the oxygen level,
and the temperature.

00:27:11.530 --> 00:27:13.730
And over the first
four days, we can just

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:16.190
see that steadily the
oxygen is decreasing,

00:27:16.190 --> 00:27:18.390
and the carbon
dioxide is increasing.

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:19.420
And that continues.

00:27:19.420 --> 00:27:20.795
Boy, did I learn
a lot of biology

00:27:20.795 --> 00:27:22.340
when we made this video.

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:24.950
The light cycle on and
off every 12 hours.

00:27:24.950 --> 00:27:27.230
You still see the carbon
dioxide going, up even

00:27:27.230 --> 00:27:29.930
though there are leaves.

00:27:29.930 --> 00:27:31.790
And we also see that
plants grow and move

00:27:31.790 --> 00:27:34.362
at night, which I did not know.

00:27:34.362 --> 00:27:36.570
It's really not until we
get a really large selection

00:27:36.570 --> 00:27:39.120
of leaves here on the seventh
day of this germination process

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:41.920
that we first see the
carbon dioxide go down.

00:27:41.920 --> 00:27:43.530
This is a really fun question.

00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:46.590
When we tested this, we gave it
to a group of pretty advanced

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:48.390
high school students
and asked them

00:27:48.390 --> 00:27:54.000
to use this to collect evidence
that photosynthesis only

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:56.940
happens when the
lights are turned on.

00:27:56.940 --> 00:27:59.950
And boy, was that a challenge
because almost all students,

00:27:59.950 --> 00:28:02.120
rather than looking at
changes in gas level,

00:28:02.120 --> 00:28:03.480
they just looked at gas levels.

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:06.510
So is the level of carbon
dioxide higher during the day,

00:28:06.510 --> 00:28:08.910
for example, was a
question that they asked.

00:28:08.910 --> 00:28:11.800
And so we really saw that the
ability to design experiments,

00:28:11.800 --> 00:28:14.220
like what evidence do you
need to answer a question,

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:15.990
is something that
is readily available

00:28:15.990 --> 00:28:18.460
with this interactive video.

00:28:18.460 --> 00:28:20.280
So that's examples.

00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:21.840
Pivot Interactives
is the company

00:28:21.840 --> 00:28:24.333
that we have that
makes these available.

00:28:24.333 --> 00:28:25.750
But interactive
video, in general,

00:28:25.750 --> 00:28:27.680
we think is a very
powerful learning tool.

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:31.632
I look forward to the questions.

00:28:31.632 --> 00:28:32.590
SAIF RAYYAN: Thank you.

00:28:32.590 --> 00:28:33.298
Thank you, Peter.

00:28:33.298 --> 00:28:35.490
I'm always amazed
looking at your videos.

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:38.450
Some of these studies
I haven't seen before.

00:28:38.450 --> 00:28:43.720
And I love the level of
complexity of the activities

00:28:43.720 --> 00:28:45.745
that you can actually
structure around it.

00:28:45.745 --> 00:28:47.620
We have a lot of questions
in the chat, which

00:28:47.620 --> 00:28:50.950
we'll reserve till the
end of the presentations.

00:28:50.950 --> 00:28:53.140
But one quick
question, people are

00:28:53.140 --> 00:28:56.560
asking if Pivot
Interactives is free

00:28:56.560 --> 00:28:58.330
or if there is some
sort of an access

00:28:58.330 --> 00:29:01.390
for underprivileged communities.

00:29:04.600 --> 00:29:06.050
PETER BOHACEK: It's not free.

00:29:06.050 --> 00:29:08.710
We worked on a model where
we could make it for free,

00:29:08.710 --> 00:29:11.800
and we were not successful in
getting the funding that we

00:29:11.800 --> 00:29:13.690
would need to develop
the tools, both

00:29:13.690 --> 00:29:17.210
the videos and the software
platform that hosts it.

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:22.630
And so I can post in the
chat a link for the website.

00:29:22.630 --> 00:29:24.130
And we wish it were.

00:29:24.130 --> 00:29:25.480
We're a very small company.

00:29:25.480 --> 00:29:29.110
I'm a teacher, and
my business partner,

00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:32.268
who is a college professor,
started it ourselves.

00:29:32.268 --> 00:29:34.060
And if we could figure
out a way to give it

00:29:34.060 --> 00:29:36.040
to the world for free,
so if any of the people

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:38.403
here have that means
at their disposal

00:29:38.403 --> 00:29:40.570
that they can help us give
it to the world for free,

00:29:40.570 --> 00:29:41.110
we're ready.

00:29:41.110 --> 00:29:43.000
We're not attached to
the commercial model.

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:44.710
It's just the only
one we know of that

00:29:44.710 --> 00:29:46.350
allows us to scale this.

00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:47.290
SAIF RAYYAN: Great.

00:29:47.290 --> 00:29:51.610
And we'll continue
this discussion after.

00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:52.325
Thank you, Peter.

00:29:52.325 --> 00:29:52.620
PETER BOHACEK: Welcome.

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:54.037
SAIF RAYYAN: And
next, we're going

00:29:54.037 --> 00:29:59.920
to hear from Jeannie about
her experience of moving

00:29:59.920 --> 00:30:04.120
some of her labs to
become remote labs.

00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:05.090
JEANNIE TALBOT: Hello.

00:30:05.090 --> 00:30:06.830
My name's Jeannie Talbot.

00:30:06.830 --> 00:30:09.290
I am the Physics Lab
Manager at University

00:30:09.290 --> 00:30:10.790
of La Verne in California.

00:30:13.540 --> 00:30:15.160
Let me share my screen.

00:30:20.400 --> 00:30:26.160
So I am using a tool that
someone else created.

00:30:26.160 --> 00:30:30.750
I use LabsLand, and
this is a recent use.

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:34.200
We had two weeks prep time.

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:37.860
How are we going to get
labs online in two weeks?

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:41.040
Nobody from my department had
ever done remote labs before.

00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:42.510
Some of the other
departments were

00:30:42.510 --> 00:30:44.980
using simulations, but those--

00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:47.220
that platform wasn't
going to work for physics

00:30:47.220 --> 00:30:50.040
because of content issues and
also because they weren't--

00:30:52.750 --> 00:31:00.100
they didn't teach the how
to set up an experiment,

00:31:00.100 --> 00:31:04.310
like Peter was talking about
and John touched on, as well.

00:31:04.310 --> 00:31:07.750
So after my frantic
googling, I found LabsLand.

00:31:07.750 --> 00:31:09.130
I was really impressed.

00:31:09.130 --> 00:31:14.615
I was originally
drawn to the content.

00:31:14.615 --> 00:31:16.490
They had the content
that we wanted to teach.

00:31:16.490 --> 00:31:19.820
But this idea of having
a real laboratory

00:31:19.820 --> 00:31:23.180
somewhere in the world
controlled via the internet

00:31:23.180 --> 00:31:26.810
was really catchy.

00:31:26.810 --> 00:31:29.750
So this is not all of the
things that they have,

00:31:29.750 --> 00:31:32.240
but this is a sample.

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:35.240
The electronics labs
was really crucial

00:31:35.240 --> 00:31:41.810
because second semester,
physics labs are needed.

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:45.710
We have to teach them how to
use certain equipment in order

00:31:45.710 --> 00:31:50.120
for students to move on
and go to advanced labs.

00:31:50.120 --> 00:31:52.100
And they had the
equipment that we needed

00:31:52.100 --> 00:31:54.800
to train our students to use.

00:31:54.800 --> 00:31:58.490
This is a screenshot
basically of the breadboard

00:31:58.490 --> 00:32:01.250
that you can use in
the electronics lab.

00:32:01.250 --> 00:32:05.330
So all these pieces on
the breadboard, students

00:32:05.330 --> 00:32:07.670
can move them around, and
they can move the wires.

00:32:07.670 --> 00:32:13.670
They can set up the pieces
that they want to put.

00:32:13.670 --> 00:32:14.810
So they have some agency.

00:32:14.810 --> 00:32:17.010
They can set up
their own experiment.

00:32:17.010 --> 00:32:21.710
So if we ask them to come
up with a relationship

00:32:21.710 --> 00:32:27.820
of the electricity flowing,
the voltage flowing

00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:29.680
through a capacitor
in an RC circuit,

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:31.390
they can come up with their own.

00:32:31.390 --> 00:32:33.790
And they can move around
the parts and try it out.

00:32:33.790 --> 00:32:38.830
We have a whole
bunch of equipment

00:32:38.830 --> 00:32:40.510
that they can look at.

00:32:40.510 --> 00:32:43.210
They have multimeters,
function generators.

00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:47.200
They have oscilloscopes
and a DC power supply.

00:32:47.200 --> 00:32:49.090
And they can change
all the settings

00:32:49.090 --> 00:32:50.800
on all of these things.

00:32:50.800 --> 00:32:55.270
And learning to use these
pieces of equipment, especially

00:32:55.270 --> 00:33:00.200
an oscilloscope, was really
crucial to our program

00:33:00.200 --> 00:33:03.840
and how we were expecting
them to move on.

00:33:03.840 --> 00:33:06.950
So the way that I set
it up within two weeks

00:33:06.950 --> 00:33:08.960
was I just played
with their equipment

00:33:08.960 --> 00:33:12.350
until I came up with
lab experiments.

00:33:12.350 --> 00:33:16.400
And then I had to write
my own lab manuals.

00:33:16.400 --> 00:33:19.820
There are some
resources out there

00:33:19.820 --> 00:33:22.550
that don't make you
come up with anything.

00:33:22.550 --> 00:33:24.097
You just take the equipment.

00:33:24.097 --> 00:33:26.180
You take the program and
send it to your students.

00:33:26.180 --> 00:33:28.910
And all you have to
mess with is grades.

00:33:28.910 --> 00:33:31.333
This one, you have
a lot more control,

00:33:31.333 --> 00:33:33.500
but you also have to do the
work of putting together

00:33:33.500 --> 00:33:36.920
a manual, which I really
appreciated because it gave us

00:33:36.920 --> 00:33:43.820
the flexibility to decide what
we needed for our students.

00:33:43.820 --> 00:33:46.790
We ran three labs.

00:33:46.790 --> 00:33:49.550
Two of them were
circuits labs, and one

00:33:49.550 --> 00:33:51.920
was a radioactivity lab.

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:57.680
And the students were able to
do all of these experiments

00:33:57.680 --> 00:34:02.740
almost the same way that
we would do them in person.

00:34:02.740 --> 00:34:08.620
We still held video conferences
to have optional lab meetings.

00:34:08.620 --> 00:34:10.690
And about half the
students showed up.

00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:14.659
We didn't make it mandatory
because these are hard times.

00:34:14.659 --> 00:34:17.739
And so in real-time,
if students set up

00:34:17.739 --> 00:34:20.170
a problematic
breadboard, they could

00:34:20.170 --> 00:34:24.100
ask for advice in real-time,
and that was really useful.

00:34:24.100 --> 00:34:26.500
All the people would have
their own experiment up

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:28.905
on their own computers
and ask for advice.

00:34:28.905 --> 00:34:30.280
And they could
share their screen

00:34:30.280 --> 00:34:32.620
and whatnot and get advice.

00:34:32.620 --> 00:34:34.570
The students who didn't
show up for those

00:34:34.570 --> 00:34:37.480
had to do this through
email, but they were still

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:40.670
able to complete it
on their own time.

00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:42.670
This was a big deal
for my department.

00:34:42.670 --> 00:34:47.469
Our grading system is based
on lab notebooks for labs,

00:34:47.469 --> 00:34:51.159
and that didn't have
to change for this.

00:34:51.159 --> 00:34:53.679
We still use the same format.

00:34:53.679 --> 00:34:57.250
Some of the students wrote
in their own lab notebook

00:34:57.250 --> 00:34:59.770
from in-person labs,
and some of them

00:34:59.770 --> 00:35:03.070
wrote in digital documents.

00:35:03.070 --> 00:35:05.020
But we still had
the same rubric,

00:35:05.020 --> 00:35:08.440
and we graded it the same way.

00:35:08.440 --> 00:35:14.090
So that continuity was very
useful for my department.

00:35:14.090 --> 00:35:17.740
So what we really
appreciated about labs

00:35:17.740 --> 00:35:19.570
LabsLand was that it
was really consistent.

00:35:19.570 --> 00:35:25.090
It created a format
that we could

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:30.790
keep most of our same lab
goals, and lab formats,

00:35:30.790 --> 00:35:36.080
and grading, and structure
and just put it online.

00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:37.870
We were able to train
them on equipment

00:35:37.870 --> 00:35:43.600
that we need them to know
how to use in further years.

00:35:43.600 --> 00:35:45.490
And also, we were
able to ask them

00:35:45.490 --> 00:35:48.617
questions like, if
you wanted to see

00:35:48.617 --> 00:35:50.200
the relationship
between these things,

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:51.940
how would you set this up?

00:35:51.940 --> 00:35:55.390
And they would have the choices
to be able to put that together

00:35:55.390 --> 00:35:57.830
in this environment.

00:35:57.830 --> 00:36:00.430
The other thing that I really
appreciated was the real data.

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:06.900
The real data doesn't
match the theoretical.

00:36:06.900 --> 00:36:10.710
And so it's-- they
were able to see that,

00:36:10.710 --> 00:36:13.380
because they're looking
at a real oscilloscope.

00:36:13.380 --> 00:36:18.510
And so they can find the
frequencies, et cetera,

00:36:18.510 --> 00:36:25.170
that just don't quite
match with theory

00:36:25.170 --> 00:36:28.920
and then consider what
might be happening

00:36:28.920 --> 00:36:32.600
and think about what's
going on in a real lab

00:36:32.600 --> 00:36:36.890
to create these discrepancies.

00:36:36.890 --> 00:36:39.080
And for next time,
we are planning

00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:44.960
to allow them to set up their
own experiments a little more.

00:36:44.960 --> 00:36:49.970
In the interest of time, we
gave them canned experiments

00:36:49.970 --> 00:36:56.990
because that was fast, but
we are able to think about--

00:36:56.990 --> 00:36:59.360
so we could tell
the students, hey,

00:36:59.360 --> 00:37:05.360
so you need to come up with a
relationship between distance

00:37:05.360 --> 00:37:14.390
and the radioactive counts or
the thickness of the barrier

00:37:14.390 --> 00:37:17.240
and compared to
radioactive counts.

00:37:17.240 --> 00:37:19.100
How would you set
up this experiment?

00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:22.400
And then they could just do
that based on what is available.

00:37:29.030 --> 00:37:33.690
And so that's all I've
got on that front.

00:37:33.690 --> 00:37:40.757
And I am looking forward to
whatever people might ask.

00:37:40.757 --> 00:37:42.090
SAIF RAYYAN: Thank you, Jeannie.

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:44.020
That's a great presentation.

00:37:44.020 --> 00:37:48.960
I really like how you
emphasized the fact

00:37:48.960 --> 00:37:53.520
that you have to engage with
activities that are provided

00:37:53.520 --> 00:37:54.630
by a certain product.

00:37:54.630 --> 00:37:58.502
I know that there are a
lot of products out there.

00:37:58.502 --> 00:38:00.960
If you have a package and you
just send it to the students,

00:38:00.960 --> 00:38:02.520
you have no idea
what they're doing.

00:38:02.520 --> 00:38:05.730
It's very hard to align
to your own goals.

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:10.290
So you still have to spend a
lot of time trying to figure out

00:38:10.290 --> 00:38:13.830
pedagogically how to get
the learning goals that you

00:38:13.830 --> 00:38:16.270
aim for using the product.

00:38:16.270 --> 00:38:19.980
And I think what you provided
is a great example of how

00:38:19.980 --> 00:38:21.840
that can work.

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:23.520
And that leads us--
a lot of questions

00:38:23.520 --> 00:38:27.810
that are coming up, which
we'll ask the speakers later.

00:38:27.810 --> 00:38:30.780
But several people are
asking about biology.

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:34.110
So, Bruce, can you
can you tell us

00:38:34.110 --> 00:38:39.630
more about your experience
with virtual manufacturing?

00:38:39.630 --> 00:38:41.250
BRUCE VAN DYKE: Yeah, of course.

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:44.510
Let me get some screen up here.

00:38:44.510 --> 00:38:45.010
All right.

00:38:45.010 --> 00:38:50.670
So I'm the Director, Founder and
Director of the Biotechnology

00:38:50.670 --> 00:38:54.630
and Good Manufacturing
program at Quincy College

00:38:54.630 --> 00:38:56.460
in Quincy, Mass.

00:38:56.460 --> 00:38:59.250
We're just a few
miles south of Boston.

00:38:59.250 --> 00:39:03.360
And our goal is to
train people to work

00:39:03.360 --> 00:39:07.020
in the biopharmaceutical
industry as entry level

00:39:07.020 --> 00:39:08.430
positions.

00:39:08.430 --> 00:39:12.090
And that includes
gene and cell therapy,

00:39:12.090 --> 00:39:13.860
which have come on
board very strongly

00:39:13.860 --> 00:39:17.410
over the last few months.

00:39:17.410 --> 00:39:22.650
And so we've developed, over the
last several years, a program

00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:27.890
