others you can see.

00:51:06.490 --> 00:51:10.760
He built this for a lab
at MIT, this X-ray lab.

00:51:10.760 --> 00:51:12.900
And you can see
classical mechanics

00:51:12.900 --> 00:51:14.240
for doing golf and so forth.

00:51:14.240 --> 00:51:17.800
There's a lot of resources
here that he's built out.

00:51:17.800 --> 00:51:20.140
And he and I have been working
together, as I mentioned,

00:51:20.140 --> 00:51:22.010
for about 15 years.

00:51:22.010 --> 00:51:23.430
So there's a lot to be done.

00:51:23.430 --> 00:51:24.430
And we're still working.

00:51:24.430 --> 00:51:26.140
We're working on
augmented reality

00:51:26.140 --> 00:51:29.720
right now for some of these
virtual labs that we're doing.

00:51:29.720 --> 00:51:33.460
And let me show you
one other resource that

00:51:33.460 --> 00:51:35.260
would be very useful.

00:51:35.260 --> 00:51:37.690
Anyway, I can't
find it right now,

00:51:37.690 --> 00:51:39.890
so I won't be able to
share that with you.

00:51:39.890 --> 00:51:43.300
But there is an organization.

00:51:43.300 --> 00:51:50.800
It's called InnovATE with
A-T-E in capitals, InnovATEBIO.

00:51:50.800 --> 00:51:55.780
It is the National
Science Foundation

00:51:55.780 --> 00:51:59.590
Center for Biotechnology.

00:51:59.590 --> 00:52:03.300
And so I've done a couple
webinars on their website.

00:52:03.300 --> 00:52:07.540
We'll put those-- give
those resources, too,

00:52:07.540 --> 00:52:10.180
so make those resources
available to you.

00:52:10.180 --> 00:52:12.480
And there is all
kinds of tools there

00:52:12.480 --> 00:52:15.010
from things that are useful
for high school students

00:52:15.010 --> 00:52:17.680
or any level of
college students.

00:52:17.680 --> 00:52:20.770
And so it's highly functional.

00:52:20.770 --> 00:52:23.860
All of them are
free, open source.

00:52:23.860 --> 00:52:27.880
And you'll be able to find
the webinar I did for what

00:52:27.880 --> 00:52:29.280
I've just been showing you now.

00:52:29.280 --> 00:52:32.420
And I did another one
on protein purification,

00:52:32.420 --> 00:52:37.090
which is not our virtual lab,
but it was built by AG Booth.

00:52:37.090 --> 00:52:40.660
He's a professor at Leeds
University in London.

00:52:40.660 --> 00:52:43.900
And he developed
this program in 1998.

00:52:43.900 --> 00:52:47.955
It's powerful for teaching
protein purification.

00:52:47.955 --> 00:52:49.330
And so there's a
webinar up there

00:52:49.330 --> 00:52:52.780
for that, as well, if that
would be interesting and many

00:52:52.780 --> 00:52:53.830
other site types.

00:52:53.830 --> 00:52:56.460
So I look forward
to your questions.

00:52:56.460 --> 00:52:58.180
It's exciting to be here.

00:52:58.180 --> 00:53:01.882
And thank you for listening.

00:53:01.882 --> 00:53:02.882
KIRKY DELONG: Thank you.

00:53:02.882 --> 00:53:05.400
SAIF RAYYAN: Thank you, Bruce.

00:53:05.400 --> 00:53:06.900
I'll hand it over to Kirky.

00:53:06.900 --> 00:53:09.325
You have a lot of interesting
questions, great questions,

00:53:09.325 --> 00:53:11.000
and I'm looking forward
to the discussion.

00:53:11.000 --> 00:53:11.830
KIRKY DELONG: Great questions.

00:53:11.830 --> 00:53:14.350
I'm going to start with some
questions for anybody just

00:53:14.350 --> 00:53:16.277
to get these out there.

00:53:16.277 --> 00:53:18.610
Towards the end of this,
there's been a lot of questions

00:53:18.610 --> 00:53:20.740
about the future plans.

00:53:20.740 --> 00:53:22.830
Is the experience
going online going

00:53:22.830 --> 00:53:26.500
to change how you
teach going forward?

00:53:26.500 --> 00:53:27.910
They understand
the need to react

00:53:27.910 --> 00:53:29.530
in the current situations.

00:53:29.530 --> 00:53:32.770
But if similar constraints come
up in the fall, what are you

00:53:32.770 --> 00:53:33.408
planning for?

00:53:33.408 --> 00:53:35.950
What might you do differently
in the fall with the extra time

00:53:35.950 --> 00:53:40.280
to plan and learn from what
you've done this spring,

00:53:40.280 --> 00:53:41.230
anybody?

00:53:41.230 --> 00:53:42.230
JOHN LIU: Sure.

00:53:42.230 --> 00:53:45.450
I can offer two or three things.

00:53:45.450 --> 00:53:48.170
The first thing is
now-- first of all, now

00:53:48.170 --> 00:53:52.280
after seeing-- trying it once,
I think the instructors have all

00:53:52.280 --> 00:53:55.970
seen how important it is to
start with the question, what

00:53:55.970 --> 00:53:57.740
are your learning
goals, and then

00:53:57.740 --> 00:54:00.650
to go through that whole loop.

00:54:00.650 --> 00:54:02.960
We've all seen it
in one iteration,

00:54:02.960 --> 00:54:06.250
but now we've seen what
works and what doesn't.

00:54:06.250 --> 00:54:10.400
Often, it's easy to just get
caught up with this transition.

00:54:10.400 --> 00:54:12.440
This is what I can do,
or that is what I can do.

00:54:12.440 --> 00:54:14.240
But if you start
with that question,

00:54:14.240 --> 00:54:16.850
it will lead you to
a very good path.

00:54:16.850 --> 00:54:19.430
The second thing is it's
actually very difficult,

00:54:19.430 --> 00:54:24.350
to be honest, to say to plan
for the fall, because MIT,

00:54:24.350 --> 00:54:26.510
and I would assume most
institutions right now,

00:54:26.510 --> 00:54:30.770
haven't actually decided on what
the fall is going to look like.

00:54:30.770 --> 00:54:34.400
And at MIT, we're really
considering the full range

00:54:34.400 --> 00:54:37.820
almost, as students on
campus, to partially,

00:54:37.820 --> 00:54:41.180
to completely students
not on campus.

00:54:41.180 --> 00:54:45.770
But what I can say is that
around virtual collaboration,

00:54:45.770 --> 00:54:50.840
so as you can see, students
could see the experiments much

00:54:50.840 --> 00:54:54.560
more if the model was on
campus, and they could see it

00:54:54.560 --> 00:54:58.250
from their screen, or
collaboration around Dropbox

00:54:58.250 --> 00:54:59.900
or other software packages.

00:54:59.900 --> 00:55:02.480
Those often would
tighten the loop

00:55:02.480 --> 00:55:04.790
of how people work together.

00:55:04.790 --> 00:55:07.640
There's other areas in
analysis and simulation

00:55:07.640 --> 00:55:11.720
where instructors, we were
finding that students could

00:55:11.720 --> 00:55:15.890
learn a lot more deeply because
they could see much more not

00:55:15.890 --> 00:55:19.310
obfuscated by the
physical model.

00:55:19.310 --> 00:55:21.980
In simulations, you can see
a lot more the direct link

00:55:21.980 --> 00:55:25.070
between cause and
effect, input and output.

00:55:25.070 --> 00:55:27.740
And so that was also a
learning that we wanted

00:55:27.740 --> 00:55:31.100
to implement in the fall.

00:55:31.100 --> 00:55:33.590
KIRKY DELONG: Anybody else?

00:55:33.590 --> 00:55:35.390
PETER BOHACEK: Two
things I've noticed.

00:55:35.390 --> 00:55:37.940
One, I think that there's an
increased need for student

00:55:37.940 --> 00:55:42.200
feedback that in the past, as
we interacted with our students

00:55:42.200 --> 00:55:45.640
in a face to face, there's
lots of casual feedback

00:55:45.640 --> 00:55:48.140
that you just walk by a student
that's working on something.

00:55:48.140 --> 00:55:49.557
And you notice
what they're doing,

00:55:49.557 --> 00:55:50.780
or maybe you offer a comment.

00:55:50.780 --> 00:55:53.028
But I really acutely
notice this difference

00:55:53.028 --> 00:55:55.070
where you can't see them,
and they can't see you.

00:55:55.070 --> 00:55:57.180
And so you can't see their work.

00:55:57.180 --> 00:56:00.230
And I think as an instructor,
but I think also as a student,

00:56:00.230 --> 00:56:01.370
it's disconcerting.

00:56:01.370 --> 00:56:03.350
And so I think
coming up with ways

00:56:03.350 --> 00:56:06.020
to be able to give frequent
feedback, hopefully

00:56:06.020 --> 00:56:09.088
automated feedback, I think
as these systems are virtual,

00:56:09.088 --> 00:56:11.380
and as they develop, if we
can come up with better ways

00:56:11.380 --> 00:56:12.650
that as the students
are working,

00:56:12.650 --> 00:56:15.025
it can give them hints about
whether they're doing things

00:56:15.025 --> 00:56:16.610
right.

00:56:16.610 --> 00:56:17.840
And then just being--

00:56:17.840 --> 00:56:20.570
I have found that my
attention has turned towards,

00:56:20.570 --> 00:56:22.820
when students are working
something, like I look at it

00:56:22.820 --> 00:56:24.210
and write to them.

00:56:24.210 --> 00:56:26.240
And that feedback
seems to really help.

00:56:26.240 --> 00:56:27.950
So that's one
thing I've noticed.

00:56:27.950 --> 00:56:30.290
And the other is I
think that there's still

00:56:30.290 --> 00:56:34.700
a need for better tools to allow
students to collaborate freely.

00:56:34.700 --> 00:56:37.940
So for example, breakout
rooms, that sort of thing

00:56:37.940 --> 00:56:38.697
is the right idea.

00:56:38.697 --> 00:56:40.280
I don't know if we're
quite there yet.

00:56:40.280 --> 00:56:42.680
I just think we need
more work on how

00:56:42.680 --> 00:56:44.780
students can
spontaneously group or can

00:56:44.780 --> 00:56:46.340
be grouped by instructors.

00:56:46.340 --> 00:56:50.810
I really acutely feel that
missing in my own instruction,

00:56:50.810 --> 00:56:54.050
students being able to work
in groups or two or three

00:56:54.050 --> 00:56:55.070
or share across groups.

00:56:55.070 --> 00:56:56.990
This group of two or
three hears something

00:56:56.990 --> 00:56:58.698
or sees something
another group is doing.

00:56:58.698 --> 00:57:01.905
We need better tools for that.

00:57:01.905 --> 00:57:03.996
KIRKY DELONG: Bruce?

00:57:03.996 --> 00:57:07.760
BRUCE VAN DYKE: So what
we've been looking at

00:57:07.760 --> 00:57:12.500
are trying to develop more
resources for this being

00:57:12.500 --> 00:57:17.360
able to actually evaluate
how well the students are

00:57:17.360 --> 00:57:20.240
able to learn from
the virtual labs.

00:57:20.240 --> 00:57:23.210
And we're working intensively
on that in the background

00:57:23.210 --> 00:57:26.570
with that National Science
Foundation training

00:57:26.570 --> 00:57:29.310
organization I mentioned to you.

00:57:29.310 --> 00:57:34.400
So we have some assessments
up there, but there--

00:57:34.400 --> 00:57:37.315
takes a lot of time
to build them out.

00:57:37.315 --> 00:57:42.578
And that takes money, which
is hard to get for people.

00:57:42.578 --> 00:57:44.120
It's easy to get a
lot of grant money

00:57:44.120 --> 00:57:46.440
for equipment and
supplies and so forth,

00:57:46.440 --> 00:57:49.460
but to get money for people
is sometimes challenging.

00:57:49.460 --> 00:57:52.820
But we have now met
this other organization.

00:57:52.820 --> 00:57:54.470
Well, it's going to
help us build out

00:57:54.470 --> 00:57:56.330
a lot of those type resources.

00:57:56.330 --> 00:57:57.920
Also, we track people.

00:57:57.920 --> 00:58:01.020
Our programs can track what
the students are doing,

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:02.300
if they're--

00:58:02.300 --> 00:58:06.320
how active they are
on the virtual labs,

00:58:06.320 --> 00:58:08.740
whether or not they're having
to repeat over and over

00:58:08.740 --> 00:58:11.240
or whether or not they're
catching things quickly

00:58:11.240 --> 00:58:14.570
and being able to move through.

00:58:14.570 --> 00:58:16.460
And another thing
is something we've

00:58:16.460 --> 00:58:18.600
been thinking about
for about three years,

00:58:18.600 --> 00:58:21.740
and we're actually working
on now, is just what Peter

00:58:21.740 --> 00:58:25.430
was just talking
about, a build out room

00:58:25.430 --> 00:58:27.170
where you can see everybody.

00:58:27.170 --> 00:58:29.780
And you can have
intense discussions

00:58:29.780 --> 00:58:30.860
about whatever it is.

00:58:30.860 --> 00:58:35.060
They can talk about what the
problems are in real-time.

00:58:35.060 --> 00:58:36.680
We actually have
industry people said

00:58:36.680 --> 00:58:38.943
they'd come in
and work with them

00:58:38.943 --> 00:58:40.610
through some of these
different aspects,

00:58:40.610 --> 00:58:42.735
as well, so that we didn't
have to do all the work.

00:58:42.735 --> 00:58:44.360
So we hope to have that.

00:58:44.360 --> 00:58:49.220
I'm working with the programmer,
Yakov Churner, and his company

00:58:49.220 --> 00:58:51.320
to try to get this
also online as quickly

00:58:51.320 --> 00:58:55.420
as we can to make it more
effective for the students

00:58:55.420 --> 00:59:00.850
to be able to work in real-time
and understand the processes

00:59:00.850 --> 00:59:03.590
that they're doing.

00:59:03.590 --> 00:59:04.590
KIRKY DELONG: Thank you.

00:59:04.590 --> 00:59:05.850
Jeannie?

00:59:05.850 --> 00:59:07.250
JEANNIE TALBOT: Thanks, Bruce.

00:59:07.250 --> 00:59:10.970
That's really good thoughts.

00:59:10.970 --> 00:59:12.488
I haven't spent
much time thinking

00:59:12.488 --> 00:59:14.030
about how we're
going to get students

00:59:14.030 --> 00:59:15.560
to interact with each other.

00:59:15.560 --> 00:59:18.650
That's something I
should probably do.

00:59:18.650 --> 00:59:30.570
I've been asked to provide
plans for going forward,

00:59:30.570 --> 00:59:36.180
not only just all the way
back to on campus labs

00:59:36.180 --> 00:59:39.570
and on campus, but we can
only have half of them

00:59:39.570 --> 00:59:42.630
in the room or a third
of them in the room.

00:59:42.630 --> 00:59:46.740
And so thinking about if
we are on campus, how we're

00:59:46.740 --> 00:59:51.960
going to keep all the
lab experiments going

00:59:51.960 --> 00:59:55.630
while only allowing them in
the room a third of the time.

00:59:55.630 --> 00:59:58.830
And also, we need to
have a plan for if we

00:59:58.830 --> 01:00:03.420
need to send them home again
or if we don't ever come back.

01:00:03.420 --> 01:00:05.310
We're working on
all these plans.

01:00:05.310 --> 01:00:08.610
And I want to reiterate
what John was saying.

01:00:08.610 --> 01:00:11.550
All of my department is putting
an emphasis on our learning

01:00:11.550 --> 01:00:14.640
goals and how we can do
better with our learning goals

01:00:14.640 --> 01:00:18.350
in all of those situations.

01:00:18.350 --> 01:00:19.680
KIRKY DELONG: Thank you.

01:00:19.680 --> 01:00:20.250
And I agree.

01:00:20.250 --> 01:00:25.380
That's very important,
especially given this,

01:00:25.380 --> 01:00:28.500
we don't know what the situation
is with students at home.

01:00:28.500 --> 01:00:31.440
One of the questions that's
come up over and over again

01:00:31.440 --> 01:00:35.370
is around, can you use
these for guided inquiry,

01:00:35.370 --> 01:00:39.000
or problem-based learning,
or project-based learning?

01:00:39.000 --> 01:00:40.710
Have anybody tried some of that?

01:00:40.710 --> 01:00:43.226
Can you share your experiences?

01:00:43.226 --> 01:00:45.550
BRUCE VAN DYKE: Well, I
don't know if it's really

01:00:45.550 --> 01:00:47.690
any different than
what I've already said,

01:00:47.690 --> 01:00:53.350
but students have these projects
that are enormous projects,

01:00:53.350 --> 01:01:02.110
to start with a vial
minus 150 in freezer,

01:01:02.110 --> 01:01:04.720
and taking that out, and
ending up with a protein,

01:01:04.720 --> 01:01:07.480
purified validated protein
product, at the end.

01:01:07.480 --> 01:01:11.612
I mean, they're huge processes
and very time-consuming.

01:01:14.140 --> 01:01:16.600
And that's basically
what the virtual lab

01:01:16.600 --> 01:01:17.680
is training them to do.

01:01:17.680 --> 01:01:19.120
That's what they
do in real-time,

01:01:19.120 --> 01:01:21.320
obviously, when
they're in the lab.

01:01:21.320 --> 01:01:24.190
So in this case,
so that virtual lab

01:01:24.190 --> 01:01:27.010
is designed to be project-based.

01:01:27.010 --> 01:01:28.660
It can be modular.

01:01:28.660 --> 01:01:33.070
If you just want to learn tissue
culture and how to grow cells,

01:01:33.070 --> 01:01:35.530
prepare cells for whatever
you want to do with them,

01:01:35.530 --> 01:01:36.640
that's there.

01:01:36.640 --> 01:01:41.860
So I haven't really been able
to think beyond that yet,

01:01:41.860 --> 01:01:45.946
but obviously there
is a big need.

01:01:45.946 --> 01:01:47.490
KIRKY DELONG: Thanks.

01:01:47.490 --> 01:01:50.880
Anybody else doing any group
work, or problem-based,

01:01:50.880 --> 01:01:52.830
or a guided inquiry
with the labs?

01:01:52.830 --> 01:01:56.760
JOHN LIU: I think one of the
iterations that we've tried

01:01:56.760 --> 01:02:02.270
is-- so I think about what I
recall is in elementary school,

01:02:02.270 --> 01:02:06.780
there's the whole put
together a PB and J sandwich.

01:02:06.780 --> 01:02:08.390
So one person has--

01:02:08.390 --> 01:02:11.940
is the hands, and the
other person is the talker.

01:02:11.940 --> 01:02:13.640
And they have to
give instructions,

01:02:13.640 --> 01:02:16.110
and the first person
just puts it together.

01:02:16.110 --> 01:02:18.450
And the first person
usually is the teacher.

01:02:18.450 --> 01:02:22.720
And so the student will say, put
the peanut butter on the bread.

01:02:22.720 --> 01:02:25.200
And it's clunk.

01:02:25.200 --> 01:02:29.600
And that kind of model
we've been exploring

01:02:29.600 --> 01:02:32.810
for guided inquiry because
what you can do then

01:02:32.810 --> 01:02:35.060
is if, for example,
the staff can

01:02:35.060 --> 01:02:36.890
be the one in front
of the experiment,

01:02:36.890 --> 01:02:38.720
they can say, OK, what--

01:02:38.720 --> 01:02:41.180
students, what do
you want me to do--

01:02:41.180 --> 01:02:43.850
turn this nob or
place this on here?

01:02:43.850 --> 01:02:47.000
And, of course, the
main learning goal

01:02:47.000 --> 01:02:50.790
isn't precise communication
or for instructions

01:02:50.790 --> 01:02:52.730
like it would be
for PB and J, but it

01:02:52.730 --> 01:02:55.230
would be guided discovery
because it's kind

01:02:55.230 --> 01:02:56.480
of like choose your adventure.

01:02:56.480 --> 01:02:57.350
You want to do this?

01:02:57.350 --> 01:02:57.850
OK.

01:02:57.850 --> 01:02:59.750
Well, then let's
continue down that rabbit

01:02:59.750 --> 01:03:03.320
hole in this particular
experiment exploration.

01:03:03.320 --> 01:03:06.045
So that's one way
we've thought about it.

01:03:06.045 --> 01:03:07.670
The other way is, as
I mentioned, kits.

01:03:07.670 --> 01:03:10.640
So if you're able
to, if you think

01:03:10.640 --> 01:03:13.430
about a particular phenomenon,
if you can break it

01:03:13.430 --> 01:03:15.740
up or simplify it,
perhaps you can actually

01:03:15.740 --> 01:03:20.300
send it over if it's safe
to the students' homes.

01:03:20.300 --> 01:03:22.430
And then you can think through.

01:03:22.430 --> 01:03:26.150
You can host a Zoom session
and walk each step through them

01:03:26.150 --> 01:03:26.912
in a Socratic way.

01:03:26.912 --> 01:03:28.370
All of them are
working through it,

01:03:28.370 --> 01:03:31.670
and they can discuss
like, oh, I saw this.

01:03:31.670 --> 01:03:35.390
And then the classmates
can converge and synthesize

01:03:35.390 --> 01:03:36.260
their observations.

01:03:36.260 --> 01:03:38.670
And then you can continue to
take them on the next step.

01:03:38.670 --> 01:03:40.250
So if the materials
can be sent home,

01:03:40.250 --> 01:03:44.150
then they also get that tactile
sense in their guided inquiry.

01:03:44.150 --> 01:03:48.050
Kirky, you also asked about
project-based learning.

01:03:48.050 --> 01:03:53.530
What we found, I mentioned the
example about play-based design

01:03:53.530 --> 01:04:00.770
and where students are putting
together their own board games.

01:04:00.770 --> 01:04:04.130
So what we found is
the learning goal here

01:04:04.130 --> 01:04:11.490
is really about closing the loop
between ideation, prototyping,

01:04:11.490 --> 01:04:17.030
user testing, and then back,
back to the second prototype,

01:04:17.030 --> 01:04:19.730
and going through
that whole loop.

01:04:19.730 --> 01:04:21.230
And so if that's
your learning goal,

01:04:21.230 --> 01:04:23.630
you don't necessarily
have to have--

01:04:23.630 --> 01:04:26.240
we do, we certainly
do have pretty --

01:04:26.240 --> 01:04:31.100
a very built out fabrication
machine shop for the toy design

01:04:31.100 --> 01:04:31.910
course.

01:04:31.910 --> 01:04:33.365
But if that's your
learning goal,

01:04:33.365 --> 01:04:35.240
there's actually a lot
of things that you can

01:04:35.240 --> 01:04:37.102
play with when you're at home.

01:04:37.102 --> 01:04:39.560
And so we found that when we're
sticking with that learning

01:04:39.560 --> 01:04:42.080
goal, we can come up
with, we send you foam,

01:04:42.080 --> 01:04:43.940
and you just cut it up
with an X-Acto knife.

01:04:43.940 --> 01:04:45.820
And we send you balsa
wood, and you can--

01:04:45.820 --> 01:04:47.030
and markers.

01:04:47.030 --> 01:04:51.890
And you can still put together a
game that tests your main ideas

01:04:51.890 --> 01:04:54.140
and allows you to
perform that loop.

01:04:54.140 --> 01:04:56.870
And so I'd offer
that as an example

01:04:56.870 --> 01:04:59.772
that perhaps you
can think through,

01:04:59.772 --> 01:05:01.980
what's the main learning
goal of your project course,

01:05:01.980 --> 01:05:04.310
and is that something that
you can transfer over?

01:05:04.310 --> 01:05:08.090
If there are areas that you
really can't, like the Segway

01:05:08.090 --> 01:05:10.460
kit, perhaps there
are ways that you

01:05:10.460 --> 01:05:13.730
can think about contracting
out the fabrication,

01:05:13.730 --> 01:05:15.230
different pieces
of the fabrication,

01:05:15.230 --> 01:05:17.090
sending them a base kit.

01:05:17.090 --> 01:05:18.770
Not everyone needs
to-- they don't all

01:05:18.770 --> 01:05:20.810
need to speck out
their own Arduino kit.

01:05:20.810 --> 01:05:23.100
You could send them all
something like that.

01:05:23.100 --> 01:05:27.020
And then some of our
classes, we're giving them

01:05:27.020 --> 01:05:29.750
a small budget that-- it
depends on, of course,

01:05:29.750 --> 01:05:32.450
also the age range
of your students.

01:05:32.450 --> 01:05:34.460
But if we had grad
students or upperclassmen,

01:05:34.460 --> 01:05:36.740
we give them a budget,
and they could manage it.

01:05:36.740 --> 01:05:39.713
Or it's you sending them
a bill of materials,

01:05:39.713 --> 01:05:41.380
and they get to compose
their materials,

01:05:41.380 --> 01:05:42.590
and you can order for them.

01:05:42.590 --> 01:05:44.673
KIRKY DELONG: Anybody else
want to make a comment?

01:05:44.673 --> 01:05:47.730
Or I'll ask the next question.

01:05:47.730 --> 01:05:48.580
OK.

01:05:48.580 --> 01:05:51.270
So the next question,
there's a group of them

01:05:51.270 --> 01:05:53.860
around the experience.

01:05:53.860 --> 01:05:56.520
What feedback are you
getting from the students

01:05:56.520 --> 01:05:59.430
about the new virtual
experiences versus what

01:05:59.430 --> 01:06:01.185
they experienced in the lab?

01:06:04.360 --> 01:06:07.390
Are the development
competencies similar?

01:06:07.390 --> 01:06:09.472
Are you seeing any
differences with that?

01:06:09.472 --> 01:06:12.950
So what are you guys seeing?

01:06:12.950 --> 01:06:13.940
Go ahead, Jeannie.

01:06:13.940 --> 01:06:16.230
JEANNIE TALBOT: Great.

01:06:16.230 --> 01:06:21.420
At La Verne, we tried to keep
it as smooth as possible.

01:06:21.420 --> 01:06:25.950
Our feedback has been
that they appreciated--

01:06:25.950 --> 01:06:28.710
not too many students
responded to the feedback.

01:06:28.710 --> 01:06:32.220
But they said that they
appreciated how consistent it

01:06:32.220 --> 01:06:35.730
was based on what we were doing
before and so that we could

01:06:35.730 --> 01:06:37.800
move through and
have the same grading

01:06:37.800 --> 01:06:39.600
policy that we
had before, and we

01:06:39.600 --> 01:06:43.140
can have the same style of lab.

01:06:43.140 --> 01:06:44.940
So that was nice.

01:06:44.940 --> 01:06:47.880
I think that was the main
comment, was the consistency.

01:06:51.730 --> 01:06:52.980
KIRKY DELONG: Go ahead, Bruce.

01:06:52.980 --> 01:06:57.410
BRUCE VAN DYKE: So what
I found was that it

01:06:57.410 --> 01:07:00.470
seems that when students--

01:07:00.470 --> 01:07:02.240
well, let me say
that after I did

01:07:02.240 --> 01:07:03.880
the webinar for
the organization,

01:07:03.880 --> 01:07:08.120
and there were
about 85 professors

01:07:08.120 --> 01:07:12.920
from around the country on
that, they all liked it,

01:07:12.920 --> 01:07:14.720
and they're working
with students on that.

01:07:14.720 --> 01:07:18.020
But what we find is that,
even though we have the step

01:07:18.020 --> 01:07:21.000
by step instructions on
the site, a lot of people

01:07:21.000 --> 01:07:24.110
don't like to read instructions.

01:07:24.110 --> 01:07:26.998
You buy something from
IKEA, and you bring it home.

01:07:26.998 --> 01:07:29.540
And you just try to assemble it
without reading instructions.

01:07:29.540 --> 01:07:32.350
Well, they go into these
virtual labs, and they try to--

01:07:32.350 --> 01:07:33.537
they want to play with them.

01:07:33.537 --> 01:07:35.870
So they just start playing
without getting instructions,

01:07:35.870 --> 01:07:37.980
and they get lost.

01:07:37.980 --> 01:07:41.670
And so we're trying to--
those that once you've

01:07:41.670 --> 01:07:45.920
instructed them and say, listen,
just before you play too much,

01:07:45.920 --> 01:07:49.045
just try stepping through it
so you understand the process.

01:07:49.045 --> 01:07:51.170
And once you've got that,
then you go back and play

01:07:51.170 --> 01:07:52.260
with them whenever you want.

01:07:52.260 --> 01:07:54.170
And when it doesn't work,
you'll know why it doesn't work,

01:07:54.170 --> 01:07:56.210
and you can go back
and readjust things.

01:07:56.210 --> 01:07:59.060
And so that's one
of the issues that

01:07:59.060 --> 01:08:01.820
has arisen as we try
to get students online.

01:08:01.820 --> 01:08:05.510
My students just do it because
they work with me all the time,

01:08:05.510 --> 01:08:08.390
whereas new people
coming into it,

01:08:08.390 --> 01:08:10.220
they get a little
lost until they get

01:08:10.220 --> 01:08:13.640
a little further instruction.

01:08:13.640 --> 01:08:17.690
KIRKY DELONG:
Peter, any comment?

01:08:17.690 --> 01:08:19.000
PETER BOHACEK: Yeah, I do.

01:08:19.000 --> 01:08:21.250
So as far as students'
opinions of them

01:08:21.250 --> 01:08:24.100
and their reaction to them,
we notice that students

01:08:24.100 --> 01:08:25.520
have different preferences.

01:08:25.520 --> 01:08:27.970
So we noticed that when students
are doing hands-on labs,

01:08:27.970 --> 01:08:32.840
that often they are worried
about whether they're doing it

01:08:32.840 --> 01:08:33.890
right, whether they are--

01:08:33.890 --> 01:08:35.390
appreciate the fact
that, if they're

01:08:35.390 --> 01:08:38.240
doing an interactive video lab
that they know they can go back

01:08:38.240 --> 01:08:41.109
and repeat their measurement
if they make a mistake.

01:08:41.109 --> 01:08:42.859
But we also notice
that there are students

01:08:42.859 --> 01:08:44.899
who say they really
like, and of course we

01:08:44.899 --> 01:08:47.569
can understand that, touching
and feeling the equipment.

01:08:47.569 --> 01:08:50.990
So as far as students' opinions
of it, we see a variety.

01:08:50.990 --> 01:08:54.319
But when we moved this
product before we started

01:08:54.319 --> 01:08:57.529
building Pivot
Interactives, we did a test

01:08:57.529 --> 01:08:59.292
to see, what are they learning?

01:08:59.292 --> 01:09:01.250
Regardless of what they
think they're learning,

01:09:01.250 --> 01:09:03.319
what are they actually
learning specifically

01:09:03.319 --> 01:09:05.750
around model building
and model evaluation,

01:09:05.750 --> 01:09:09.330
which are two of the core
things that are interested in?

01:09:09.330 --> 01:09:11.990
And so I'm going to
post in there a paper

01:09:11.990 --> 01:09:14.450
that we published
about just that,

01:09:14.450 --> 01:09:16.189
over a semester-long study.

01:09:16.189 --> 01:09:17.920
And that was for
us the green light

01:09:17.920 --> 01:09:19.670
that we should build
this because we could

01:09:19.670 --> 01:09:22.100
see that students really were
learning the kinds of science

01:09:22.100 --> 01:09:23.308
practices we hoped they were.

01:09:23.308 --> 01:09:26.026
So I'll put that in there.

01:09:26.026 --> 01:09:27.660
KIRKY DELONG: Thank you.

01:09:27.660 --> 01:09:28.180
OK.

01:09:28.180 --> 01:09:32.250
So we have a follow
on question to that.

01:09:32.250 --> 01:09:35.430
As an instructor, how can
I use one of the products

01:09:35.430 --> 01:09:38.250
and know that the students are
actually using them and not

01:09:38.250 --> 01:09:40.220
just copying?

01:09:40.220 --> 01:09:41.790
What do you guys think?

01:09:41.790 --> 01:09:44.540
JOHN LIU: Well, I
think this is why

01:09:44.540 --> 01:09:47.330
it's so important to have
a real direct connection

01:09:47.330 --> 01:09:48.788
with your students.

01:09:48.788 --> 01:09:50.330
We're actually
spending a lot of time

01:09:50.330 --> 01:09:52.160
right now discussing,
what are the best

01:09:52.160 --> 01:09:55.280
formats for assessment,
and especially

01:09:55.280 --> 01:09:57.080
as we approach the
final exam this time

01:09:57.080 --> 01:09:59.060
and thinking about fall exam.

01:09:59.060 --> 01:10:02.270
And one of the things that
has really popped out to me

01:10:02.270 --> 01:10:05.480
is the power of oral exams.

01:10:05.480 --> 01:10:08.930
As a teacher, you can probably
spend 5, 10, 15 minutes,

01:10:08.930 --> 01:10:11.060
a very short time,
with a student

01:10:11.060 --> 01:10:13.850
and get a pretty good
idea of what they do

01:10:13.850 --> 01:10:15.260
and they don't understand.

01:10:15.260 --> 01:10:17.300
And especially
during a time when

01:10:17.300 --> 01:10:19.640
the one to one, the person
to person interaction

01:10:19.640 --> 01:10:22.460
is so important,
you can consider

01:10:22.460 --> 01:10:26.420
placing in other kinds of
activities, like small group

01:10:26.420 --> 01:10:29.768
discussions or one to one
interviews on those activities

01:10:29.768 --> 01:10:32.060
so that you can have a deeper
sense of what they really

01:10:32.060 --> 01:10:33.470
do know or don't know.

01:10:33.470 --> 01:10:35.630
And you'd be surprised at
how much more seriously

01:10:35.630 --> 01:10:39.870
students take it when you
actually are going to say, hey,

01:10:39.870 --> 01:10:42.110
I'm going to follow
up with you in person.

01:10:42.110 --> 01:10:45.650
That really forces them or
motivates them to really think

01:10:45.650 --> 01:10:47.000
that they know, because if--

01:10:47.000 --> 01:10:49.160
they can hide behind
an assignment.

01:10:49.160 --> 01:10:51.650
But they can't really
hide in front of you.

01:10:51.650 --> 01:10:54.050
I did also want to
circle back to, Kirky,

01:10:54.050 --> 01:10:55.760
your previous question.

01:10:55.760 --> 01:10:58.340
Just one note about
the student experience.

01:11:01.062 --> 01:11:02.270
We're Mechanical Engineering.

01:11:02.270 --> 01:11:04.760
We probably have one of the
most hands-on activities

01:11:04.760 --> 01:11:06.010
in the school.

01:11:06.010 --> 01:11:08.720
I think about half
of our courses,

01:11:08.720 --> 01:11:13.010
and we have 500 undergrads
and 400 grad students.

01:11:13.010 --> 01:11:16.400
So there was definitely a
sense of loss in the beginning

01:11:16.400 --> 01:11:19.100
where students were
saying, hey, I signed up.

01:11:19.100 --> 01:11:22.970
I enrolled into MIT for this
course, to build a robot

01:11:22.970 --> 01:11:24.230
or to build.

01:11:24.230 --> 01:11:30.440
And so we had to manage that in
a way that, one, to demonstrate

01:11:30.440 --> 01:11:32.780
that we feel them
as instructors.

01:11:32.780 --> 01:11:35.580
We really do cherish
hands-on activities.

01:11:35.580 --> 01:11:39.590
That's partly the reason
why a lot of us are there.

01:11:39.590 --> 01:11:41.810
And two, I think this
is really important

01:11:41.810 --> 01:11:45.050
to consider, to not think of
your students as a consumer

01:11:45.050 --> 01:11:47.990
but to think of them as a
partner in the transition.

01:11:47.990 --> 01:11:50.240
We surveyed them right
in the beginning.

01:11:50.240 --> 01:11:53.090
And we said-- across
many of our courses.

01:11:53.090 --> 01:11:55.340
And we asked them, what
are you going to miss?

01:11:55.340 --> 01:11:57.680
What are the things that
you really want to get?

01:11:57.680 --> 01:12:01.290
For example, in my class, we
were just in the middle of--

01:12:01.290 --> 01:12:04.700
we were just about to start
manufacturing the yo-yos.

01:12:04.700 --> 01:12:09.320
And a lot of them said, I
don't have any feel of my yo-yo

01:12:09.320 --> 01:12:11.240
even though I spent so
much time designing it.

01:12:11.240 --> 01:12:14.930
And so in the last week
that we had on campus,

01:12:14.930 --> 01:12:18.200
the technical instructors were
spending 10 plus hour days

01:12:18.200 --> 01:12:20.150
just pumping out
different yo-yo parts so

01:12:20.150 --> 01:12:21.800
that we could send
them to students.

01:12:21.800 --> 01:12:23.990
So at least they had--

01:12:23.990 --> 01:12:25.610
they could hold
onto something that

01:12:25.610 --> 01:12:27.560
represented their design work.

01:12:27.560 --> 01:12:29.870
And then in the midway
in the transition,

01:12:29.870 --> 01:12:31.280
we pulled them again.

01:12:31.280 --> 01:12:34.700
And we can iterate,
reiterate on that process.

01:12:34.700 --> 01:12:37.640
We're a big fan of continuous
improvement in manufacturing.

01:12:37.640 --> 01:12:40.160
That's the concept we teach,
and we can apply that also

01:12:40.160 --> 01:12:42.785
in our teaching.

01:12:42.785 --> 01:12:44.570
KIRKY DELONG: Thank you.

01:12:44.570 --> 01:12:47.255
Anybody else?

01:12:47.255 --> 01:12:50.480
JEANNIE TALBOT: So
this is not as--

01:12:50.480 --> 01:12:53.900
I think following up in person
is a really, really good way

01:12:53.900 --> 01:12:55.310
to do it.

01:12:55.310 --> 01:12:56.840
Our lab instructors
have been trying

01:12:56.840 --> 01:12:59.880
to do that with all
our students, for sure.

01:12:59.880 --> 01:13:02.840
Another thing that we
do with the LabsLand

01:13:02.840 --> 01:13:10.580
is that we are alerted whenever
a student completes or attempts

01:13:10.580 --> 01:13:12.017
one of the activities.

01:13:12.017 --> 01:13:13.850
And so we can see that
they've attempted it.

01:13:13.850 --> 01:13:15.350
So if they turn
in a lab notebook,

01:13:15.350 --> 01:13:17.480
and we can see that they
haven't even done it,

01:13:17.480 --> 01:13:20.300
then we can have a
discussion with the student.

01:13:20.300 --> 01:13:24.610
But also, that allows us to see,
well, you've done the activity,

01:13:24.610 --> 01:13:26.660
but you haven't turned
in the lab notebook.

01:13:26.660 --> 01:13:30.536
And so we can talk to that
student about what's going on.

01:13:30.536 --> 01:13:34.056
KIRKY DELONG: Bruce,
did you have a comment?

01:13:34.056 --> 01:13:37.200
BRUCE VAN DYKE: Well, as I
mentioned, we can monitor them.

01:13:37.200 --> 01:13:38.280
I can.

01:13:38.280 --> 01:13:43.590
Other people using the program
cannot because it's not

01:13:43.590 --> 01:13:45.660
available for other people
to see the activity.

01:13:45.660 --> 01:13:49.650
It's only available through
the programmer and his company.

01:13:49.650 --> 01:13:51.990
And since he and I
interact all the time,

01:13:51.990 --> 01:13:54.750
I get feedback on
that all the time.

01:13:54.750 --> 01:13:58.710
I do know that we've gotten
a lot of positive feedback

01:13:58.710 --> 01:14:06.420
on people using this as to being
able to assess whether or not

01:14:06.420 --> 01:14:08.820
my students, especially
my advanced students,

01:14:08.820 --> 01:14:10.490
whether or not they
really picked up

01:14:10.490 --> 01:14:12.030
the understanding
of the process.

01:14:15.540 --> 01:14:18.990
Their exams are quite long
there, for them there,

01:14:18.990 --> 01:14:20.800
because it's all about
process, and they

01:14:20.800 --> 01:14:22.500
have to go through all that.

01:14:25.680 --> 01:14:28.590
We've got finals next week.

01:14:28.590 --> 01:14:30.930
And so they've actually
already had some lab

01:14:30.930 --> 01:14:33.850
practical finals not in the lab.

01:14:33.850 --> 01:14:36.510
And they've been online.

01:14:36.510 --> 01:14:42.660
So it's still a challenge to be
able to assess, apart from just

01:14:42.660 --> 01:14:44.550
the exams that I send.

01:14:44.550 --> 01:14:47.220
And, of course, we don't
have and didn't have time

01:14:47.220 --> 01:14:48.990
to set it up so
that you can prove

01:14:48.990 --> 01:14:51.480
they're not cheating
because they've

01:14:51.480 --> 01:14:54.480
got all the books with them.

01:14:54.480 --> 01:14:56.790
I know they have a
lot of integrity,

01:14:56.790 --> 01:14:59.700
the students that I
work with, but again,

01:14:59.700 --> 01:15:01.740
for this first round,
we just have to--

01:15:01.740 --> 01:15:05.500
we're winging it to try
to get the assessment.

01:15:05.500 --> 01:15:09.030
But for the fall if this
happens again, we are--

01:15:09.030 --> 01:15:12.420
as I mentioned, we're really
trying to build out ways

01:15:12.420 --> 01:15:15.490
to be able to test
them in real-time,

01:15:15.490 --> 01:15:21.300
such as what John talked
about, face to face telling me

01:15:21.300 --> 01:15:24.885
what they understand
about a certain process.

01:15:24.885 --> 01:15:26.930
KIRKY DELONG: Thank you.

01:15:26.930 --> 01:15:31.850
We had a question
directed at Peter.

01:15:31.850 --> 01:15:35.540
Have you thought about creating
a virtual workshop for teachers

01:15:35.540 --> 01:15:38.630
to play with the
tools and teach them

01:15:38.630 --> 01:15:43.456
how to create their
own similar labs?

01:15:43.456 --> 01:15:45.310
PETER BOHACEK:
That's a great idea.

01:15:45.310 --> 01:15:45.810
Yes.

01:15:49.920 --> 01:15:51.703
To make their own
similar labs, like one

01:15:51.703 --> 01:15:54.120
of the things I've always
wanted to share but never really

01:15:54.120 --> 01:15:57.000
had a forum to do it is
how to make these videos,

01:15:57.000 --> 01:15:57.900
because it's hard.

01:15:57.900 --> 01:16:00.367
And I don't mind
sharing about that.

01:16:00.367 --> 01:16:01.950
There certainly are
an infinite number

01:16:01.950 --> 01:16:04.530
of things that can
be recorded and never

01:16:04.530 --> 01:16:06.110
even scratch the surface.

01:16:06.110 --> 01:16:08.880
But we haven't ever really done
that, but I would invite people

01:16:08.880 --> 01:16:10.380
if they want to
contact me directly,

01:16:10.380 --> 01:16:12.030
and they have
specific questions.

01:16:12.030 --> 01:16:15.580
As far as coming up with ways
specifically to use our tools,

01:16:15.580 --> 01:16:18.150
so for example, you
can use our tools

01:16:18.150 --> 01:16:20.820
to create your--
upload your own videos

01:16:20.820 --> 01:16:22.680
and build activities
around those.

01:16:22.680 --> 01:16:24.540
Our website has a fair
amount of instruction

01:16:24.540 --> 01:16:26.580
about how to do that.

01:16:26.580 --> 01:16:28.830
But I really would like to
build more of a community.

01:16:28.830 --> 01:16:30.120
It's something that
we're working on,

01:16:30.120 --> 01:16:30.990
more of a community.

01:16:30.990 --> 01:16:33.060
Of course it would be great to
make a really broad community

01:16:33.060 --> 01:16:33.890
of everybody.

01:16:33.890 --> 01:16:35.940
But I mean specifically
of our users

01:16:35.940 --> 01:16:37.860
because it would be
more focused on what

01:16:37.860 --> 01:16:38.958
you can do with our tools.

01:16:38.958 --> 01:16:41.250
And so that's a great suggestion
that we haven't really

01:16:41.250 --> 01:16:44.806
developed yet but something
that we're working on.

01:16:44.806 --> 01:16:46.620
KIRKY DELONG: Thank you.

01:16:46.620 --> 01:16:48.960
John, we had a question for you.

01:16:48.960 --> 01:16:52.950
How much tech/TA help was
available and needed to put

01:16:52.950 --> 01:16:57.240
the 30 labs online at
MIT in the two weeks?

01:16:57.240 --> 01:17:00.888
And how did you guys
think about this?

01:17:00.888 --> 01:17:02.190
JOHN LIU: We didn't sleep.

01:17:06.340 --> 01:17:09.230
It took a lot of work.

01:17:09.230 --> 01:17:11.640
At that kind of time
scale, we do not

01:17:11.640 --> 01:17:14.730
have time to hire in
or bring in extra help.

01:17:14.730 --> 01:17:17.400
It's simply whoever
is part of that team

01:17:17.400 --> 01:17:20.110
initially, you made
do with what you have.

01:17:20.110 --> 01:17:22.740
So if you had a TA,
great if you did.

01:17:22.740 --> 01:17:29.340
Most of our lab classes have
one or two TAs, I would say.

01:17:29.340 --> 01:17:31.440
For the large
project classes, we

01:17:31.440 --> 01:17:34.290
might also have two to
four technical instructors

01:17:34.290 --> 01:17:37.350
in addition to a lead
lecturer or a lead professor

01:17:37.350 --> 01:17:39.810
for that class.

01:17:39.810 --> 01:17:42.510
But in the summer,
certainly, we're

01:17:42.510 --> 01:17:44.700
thinking many faculty
take off the summer,

01:17:44.700 --> 01:17:46.950
or instructors have
nine-months appointments.

01:17:46.950 --> 01:17:49.770
So that is something that
we're considering, as well.

01:17:49.770 --> 01:17:51.420
But that's a real constraint.

01:17:51.420 --> 01:17:53.340
This is why I
shared about, what's

01:17:53.340 --> 01:17:55.830
your workload going
to be when you think

01:17:55.830 --> 01:17:57.450
about the different models?

01:17:57.450 --> 01:18:02.400
That's definitely something
you've got to think through.

01:18:02.400 --> 01:18:03.720
KIRKY DELONG: Thank you.

01:18:03.720 --> 01:18:05.810
Jeannie, similar
question to you.

01:18:05.810 --> 01:18:08.900
As you were getting
this through,

01:18:08.900 --> 01:18:13.748
what did it take to get this
all set up for the students?

01:18:13.748 --> 01:18:17.930
JEANNIE TALBOT: Well,
once I found the platform,

01:18:17.930 --> 01:18:20.030
it didn't take too much.

01:18:20.030 --> 01:18:24.800
Like I said in my slides, I
just looked at the experiments

01:18:24.800 --> 01:18:26.060
they had available.

01:18:26.060 --> 01:18:28.880
And some of the circuits
that we do in lab they

01:18:28.880 --> 01:18:30.110
didn't have available.

01:18:30.110 --> 01:18:31.790
So I looked at what they had.

01:18:31.790 --> 01:18:33.650
I messed with it until
I found something

01:18:33.650 --> 01:18:36.330
that would work
as a replacement,

01:18:36.330 --> 01:18:38.870
teach you something similar.

01:18:38.870 --> 01:18:42.470
And I wrote a lab manual
based off what we already

01:18:42.470 --> 01:18:47.120
had and told them to do it.

01:18:47.120 --> 01:18:50.540
It took about a week
to write the lab

01:18:50.540 --> 01:18:52.520
and then send it out
to the lab instructors

01:18:52.520 --> 01:18:54.890
and make sure that it
included everything that they

01:18:54.890 --> 01:18:57.920
felt we should include.

01:18:57.920 --> 01:19:02.010
And then back to testing
with our teaching assistants,

01:19:02.010 --> 01:19:05.210
they made sure that
there were no bugs.

01:19:05.210 --> 01:19:06.680
It really didn't take too long.

01:19:12.450 --> 01:19:15.490
The only thing that I
wish that we'd done better

01:19:15.490 --> 01:19:18.490
is allowed students
more decision

01:19:18.490 --> 01:19:21.871
over what was happening
with the circuits.

01:19:21.871 --> 01:19:24.290
KIRKY DELONG: Thank you.

01:19:24.290 --> 01:19:28.600
Bruce, we had a question
about VR that you mentioned

01:19:28.600 --> 01:19:31.280
and how you're doing
augmented reality.

01:19:31.280 --> 01:19:33.130
Is it a web
interface, or does it

01:19:33.130 --> 01:19:36.010
involve something more
advanced or some hardware

01:19:36.010 --> 01:19:37.300
that they have to have?

01:19:37.300 --> 01:19:40.770
BRUCE VAN DYKE: No, you
can use it on your phone.

01:19:40.770 --> 01:19:44.110
So it'll work on any device.

01:19:44.110 --> 01:19:46.490
We're not ready
to launch it yet,

01:19:46.490 --> 01:19:51.080
but you can-- the virtual
lab I was showing you,

01:19:51.080 --> 01:19:52.390
you can use it on your phone.

01:19:52.390 --> 01:19:54.080
It works on any type of device.

01:19:54.080 --> 01:19:57.430
So students aren't limited.

01:19:57.430 --> 01:20:00.250
The augmented reality
will be the same.

01:20:00.250 --> 01:20:02.440
I can demo it to
different people,

01:20:02.440 --> 01:20:04.830
but nobody can use it yet.

01:20:07.700 --> 01:20:12.220
But it will eventually
be usable on any device.

01:20:12.220 --> 01:20:15.820
But right now, it's still
in the development stages.

01:20:15.820 --> 01:20:17.600
But you'll be able to--

01:20:17.600 --> 01:20:20.890
if you're in a classroom
or wherever you are,

01:20:20.890 --> 01:20:22.960
people will be able
to interact, be

01:20:22.960 --> 01:20:26.920
able to manipulate all of
these devices, all these bio

01:20:26.920 --> 01:20:32.930
processing devices as if you're
in it, as if you're walking

01:20:32.930 --> 01:20:34.040
and you're in it.

01:20:34.040 --> 01:20:40.220
And so it's exciting still,
but we're still developing it.

01:20:40.220 --> 01:20:45.196
So it's going to take a
little more time and money.

01:20:45.196 --> 01:20:48.160
JOHN LIU: Bruce, I think it's
really exciting that you're

01:20:48.160 --> 01:20:50.560
using augmented reality
to bring, transport

01:20:50.560 --> 01:20:52.600
the student into
the environment,

01:20:52.600 --> 01:20:55.580
because especially,
even in non-COVID days,

01:20:55.580 --> 01:20:57.830
sometimes it's difficult to
bring them into commercial

01:20:57.830 --> 01:20:59.220
or research context.

01:20:59.220 --> 01:21:00.830
BRUCE VAN DYKE: Yeah.

01:21:00.830 --> 01:21:03.490
And we're actually going
to be able to take them

01:21:03.490 --> 01:21:06.760
inside the reactors,
in a sense, to be

01:21:06.760 --> 01:21:08.550
able to see what's going on.

01:21:08.550 --> 01:21:10.343
JOHN LIU: That's awesome.

01:21:10.343 --> 01:21:12.760
BRUCE VAN DYKE: We've got a
lot of ideas we're working on,

01:21:12.760 --> 01:21:14.980
and they're all very practical.

01:21:14.980 --> 01:21:18.560
They just take money to be
able to develop them fully,

01:21:18.560 --> 01:21:20.290
but we're working on that.

01:21:20.290 --> 01:21:22.740
So we've got some money from
National Science Foundation,

01:21:22.740 --> 01:21:27.940
and the State of Massachusetts,
and an organization

01:21:27.940 --> 01:21:31.110
called Nimble.

01:21:31.110 --> 01:21:34.360
So we're working
on this, not just

01:21:34.360 --> 01:21:38.625
for the biopharma but for the
gene cell therapy companies.

01:21:38.625 --> 01:21:41.410
JOHN LIU: If you're
interested in manufacturing,

01:21:41.410 --> 01:21:44.470
I just launched a MITx
course, Fundamentals

01:21:44.470 --> 01:21:46.660
of Manufacturing Processes.

01:21:46.660 --> 01:21:49.600
And we're about to release
augmented reality for that

01:21:49.600 --> 01:21:52.120
because it's really
important for us

01:21:52.120 --> 01:21:54.010
to be able to
disassemble products

01:21:54.010 --> 01:21:57.130
and to do different things about
the process and its product

01:21:57.130 --> 01:21:58.330
assembly.

01:21:58.330 --> 01:22:01.487
So if you're interested
in how-- we actually

01:22:01.487 --> 01:22:03.070
have a very similar
approach to Bruce,

01:22:03.070 --> 01:22:05.140
because we think that
augmented reality

01:22:05.140 --> 01:22:07.240
shouldn't just be
available to people

01:22:07.240 --> 01:22:09.700
who can afford $3,000
headsets, but you

01:22:09.700 --> 01:22:11.770
should be able to use
it on your cell phone.

01:22:11.770 --> 01:22:16.120
So you can roll in the edX
course for free, Fundamentals

01:22:16.120 --> 01:22:17.630
of Manufacturing Processes.

01:22:17.630 --> 01:22:20.080
And I think we're releasing
our first augmented reality

01:22:20.080 --> 01:22:22.210
experience in a few weeks.

01:22:22.210 --> 01:22:25.628
It'll be surrounding the
analysis of casting products,

01:22:25.628 --> 01:22:26.920
like pipe fittings and whatnot.

01:22:26.920 --> 01:22:29.080
But afterwards, in a
few weeks after that,

01:22:29.080 --> 01:22:31.590
then we'll be disassembling
an Amazon Fire Tablet.

01:22:31.590 --> 01:22:32.950
BRUCE VAN DYKE: Oh, great!

01:22:32.950 --> 01:22:33.970
I'd love to see it.

01:22:33.970 --> 01:22:34.965
Send me the link.

01:22:34.965 --> 01:22:35.883
JOHN LIU: I will.

01:22:35.883 --> 01:22:36.800
BRUCE VAN DYKE: Great!

01:22:36.800 --> 01:22:37.360
JOHN LIU: I'll send it now.

01:22:37.360 --> 01:22:38.836
BRUCE VAN DYKE: Great!

01:22:38.836 --> 01:22:42.130
KIRKY DELONG: Bruce,
quick follow on question.

01:22:42.130 --> 01:22:45.970
Is there a paper describing
your VR experiences?

01:22:45.970 --> 01:22:49.050
Have you written one yet?

01:22:49.050 --> 01:22:56.500
BRUCE VAN DYKE: No, but I have
a lot of information about--

01:22:56.500 --> 01:23:00.460
well, first of all, there
have been some publications

01:23:00.460 --> 01:23:03.850
about the development of
the virtual lab, what's

01:23:03.850 --> 01:23:06.790
going on behind the scenes
for all the cyber stuff

01:23:06.790 --> 01:23:08.180
and so forth.

01:23:08.180 --> 01:23:13.900
But as far as for the use
of it, we haven't actually

01:23:13.900 --> 01:23:15.040
written a paper on that.

01:23:17.983 --> 01:23:19.650
KIRKY DELONG: We're
running out of time.

01:23:19.650 --> 01:23:22.410
I'm going to do one
more question here.

01:23:22.410 --> 01:23:24.120
We have a whole
series of questions

01:23:24.120 --> 01:23:27.270
about rubric and
assessment tools

01:23:27.270 --> 01:23:29.160
and how they are
different for real

01:23:29.160 --> 01:23:33.540
versus virtual laboratories.

01:23:33.540 --> 01:23:38.580
People have mentioned face
to face tools and grading lab

01:23:38.580 --> 01:23:40.080
notebooks.

01:23:40.080 --> 01:23:43.200
What other tools are you guys
using or have you been using?

01:23:47.760 --> 01:23:51.300
BRUCE VAN DYKE: Batch records.

01:23:51.300 --> 01:23:52.750
They have to fill
them out just as

01:23:52.750 --> 01:23:55.060
if they're in the lab
every step of the way.

01:23:55.060 --> 01:23:58.390
So that's obviously
a tool that we use,

01:23:58.390 --> 01:24:02.720
and it's complex, but yeah.

01:24:02.720 --> 01:24:05.630
So the students all just sent
me their batch records and all

01:24:05.630 --> 01:24:10.310
of their data sheets and
environmental monitoring

01:24:10.310 --> 01:24:11.600
aspects and so forth.

01:24:14.564 --> 01:24:16.350
KIRKY DELONG: John?

01:24:16.350 --> 01:24:17.496
Peter?

01:24:17.496 --> 01:24:19.310
JOHN LIU: Gradescope.

01:24:19.310 --> 01:24:22.580
I was just in a
discussion about this.

01:24:22.580 --> 01:24:24.570
The nice thing
about Gradescope is

01:24:24.570 --> 01:24:28.800
that, so there's a
rubric, rubrics that are--

01:24:28.800 --> 01:24:32.010
rubric functionality
that's implemented in it.

01:24:32.010 --> 01:24:34.080
Students can upload it
within different times,

01:24:34.080 --> 01:24:39.210
and then multiple people can
grade the same assignment

01:24:39.210 --> 01:24:40.470
to get uniform grading.

01:24:40.470 --> 01:24:42.625
And then, let's
say you grade 10,

01:24:42.625 --> 01:24:44.250
and then you realize,
oh you know what,

01:24:44.250 --> 01:24:45.910
I weighted this one too much.

01:24:45.910 --> 01:24:48.310
You can go back to
the rubric, adjust it,

01:24:48.310 --> 01:24:51.320
and it will retroactively apply
across all your assignments.

01:24:51.320 --> 01:24:52.720
You do have to think about--

01:24:52.720 --> 01:24:54.480
with hands-on, you do--

01:24:54.480 --> 01:24:57.210
labs and whatnot, you do have
to rethink because your learning

01:24:57.210 --> 01:24:59.340
goals have shifted.

01:24:59.340 --> 01:25:03.150
And you've probably-- you've
hopefully strategically focused

01:25:03.150 --> 01:25:06.060
your learning goals on
the few that you really

01:25:06.060 --> 01:25:08.340
think are important and
that you want to do.

01:25:08.340 --> 01:25:11.700
And so your rubric really
should reflect that shift.

01:25:11.700 --> 01:25:13.470
KIRKY DELONG: Thank you.

01:25:13.470 --> 01:25:16.390
I think that's everything, guys.

01:25:16.390 --> 01:25:17.620
Lots of questions.

01:25:17.620 --> 01:25:19.600
Thank you, everybody,
for attending.

01:25:19.600 --> 01:25:21.330
Thank you to our
wonderful speakers.

01:25:21.330 --> 01:25:24.720
We really enjoyed learning
about your experiences,

01:25:24.720 --> 01:25:27.200
and you've given
us amazing ideas.

01:25:27.200 --> 01:25:28.830
But thank you, guys, very much.

01:25:28.830 --> 01:25:31.312
And thank you to all of
our great guest speakers.

01:25:31.312 --> 01:25:32.395
BRUCE VAN DYKE: Thank you.

01:25:32.395 --> 01:25:33.540
JOHN LIU: Thank you so much.

01:25:33.540 --> 01:25:34.050
SAIF RAYYAN: Thank you.

01:25:34.050 --> 01:25:34.800
Thank you, John.

01:25:34.800 --> 01:25:35.510
Thank you, Bruce.

01:25:35.510 --> 01:25:36.420
Thank you, Jeannie.

01:25:36.420 --> 01:25:37.010
BRUCE VAN DYKE: Nice
meeting you all.

01:25:37.010 --> 01:25:37.410
SAIF RAYYAN: Thank you, Peter.

01:25:37.410 --> 01:25:37.740
BRUCE VAN DYKE:
Great to meet you.

01:25:37.740 --> 01:25:38.190
SAIF RAYYAN: This was fantastic.

01:25:38.190 --> 01:25:40.364
BRUCE VAN DYKE: I look
forward to getting your link.

01:25:40.364 --> 01:25:42.710
JOHN LIU: Thank you very much.

